OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026", "title": "Integer multiplication below n log n", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Integer-multiplication-below-n-log-n-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "109", "family_title": "Integer multiplication below \\(n\\log n\\)", "abstract_html": "We give a deterministic algorithm that multiplies two <i>n</i>-bit integers in \\(O(n(\\lg n)^{1-\\kappa})\\) worst-case time, with \\(\\kappa=2^{-182}\\), on one fixed finite-alphabet Turing machine with a fixed finite number of one-dimensional tapes. The algorithm is exact for every input length and disproves the \\(n\\log n\\) optimality conjecture of Schönhage and Strassen in this model.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is one such machine \\(A\\) which, for every \\(n\\ge1\\), takes \\(x\\#y\\), where \\(x,y\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\), and returns \\( \\operatorname{bin}_{2n}\\bigl(\\operatorname{val}(x)\\operatorname{val}(y)\\bigr). \\) Its worst-case …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A machine is a deterministic Turing machine with one fixed finite alphabet and one fixed finite number of one-dimensional tapes. A step reads and writes at most one cell on each tape and moves each head by at most one cell.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\), \\(\\operatorname{val}(x)\\) is its nonnegative integer value, with the leftmost bit most significant.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0\\le z&lt;2^k\\), \\(\\operatorname{bin}_k(z)\\) is its binary representation padded on the left to length \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\lg n=\\max\\{\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil,1\\}\\) and \\(\\kappa=2^{-182}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is one such machine \\(A\\) which, for every \\(n\\ge1\\), takes \\(x\\#y\\), where \\(x,y\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\), and returns\n\\[\n\\operatorname{bin}_{2n}\\bigl(\\operatorname{val}(x)\\operatorname{val}(y)\\bigr).\n\\]\nIts worst-case running time is\n\\[\nT_A(n)=O\\!\\left(n(\\lg n)^{1-\\kappa}\\right).\n\\]\nThe bound holds through all input lengths.</p>", "proof": "<p>For sufficiently large \\(n\\), generate Proposition 12’s parameters, apply Proposition 14’s exact-recovery algorithm, and use Proposition 15’s complete fixed-tape time bound.</p><p>Read \\(x\\#y\\), count \\(n\\), and copy the two operands in \\(O(n)\\) time using Proposition 1’s counter conventions. All leading zeroes are retained. Proposition 14 proves the required exact \\(2n\\)-bit output, and Proposition 15 proves\n\\[\nO(n(\\log n)^{1-\\kappa}).\n\\]</p><p>Fix one cutoff beyond all construction, precision, disk-margin, and layout conditions, and use schoolbook multiplication below it. The finitely many smaller lengths are absorbed by enlarging the time constant. The resulting single machine satisfies the stated bound with \\(\\lg n=\\max\\{\\lceil\\log_2n\\rceil,1\\}\\) for every \\(n\\ge1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 2, “Exact division and integer square root.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are fixed-alphabet, fixed-finite-tape deterministic machines which perform the following tasks in \\( O\\!\\left(n(\\lg n)^{1-\\kappa}\\right) \\) worst-case time for every \\(n\\ge1\\):", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the machine model, \\(\\operatorname{val}\\), \\(\\operatorname{bin}_n\\), \\(\\lg\\), and \\(\\kappa=2^{-182}\\) of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x,y\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\), \\(a=\\operatorname{val}(x)\\), and \\(b=\\operatorname{val}(y)\\). Leading zeroes are allowed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are fixed-alphabet, fixed-finite-tape deterministic machines which perform the following tasks in\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(n(\\lg n)^{1-\\kappa}\\right)\n\\]\nworst-case time for every \\(n\\ge1\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(b&gt;0\\), division takes \\(x\\#y\\) and returns\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{bin}_n(q)\\#\\operatorname{bin}_n(r),\n   \\qquad q=\\lfloor a/b\\rfloor,\\quad r=a-bq.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Integer square root takes \\(x\\) and returns\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{bin}_n(\\lfloor\\sqrt a\\rfloor).\n   \\]</p>", "proof": "<p>A linear scan handles a zero dividend or radicand and locates significant bits. Normalize positive inputs as\n\\[\nb=2^hc,\\quad0\\le h&lt;n,\\quad1\\le c&lt;2\n\\]\nfor division, and\n\\[\na=2^{2e}c,\\quad0\\le e&lt;n/2,\\quad1\\le c&lt;4\n\\]\nfor square root. Store normalized prefixes with a marked beginning.</p><p>Take \\(P=2n+4\\). Proposition 16 computes the required approximation in \\(O(B(P))=O(B(n))\\). Its outer and inner stage counts are \\(O((\\lg n)^2)\\); their \\(O(\\lg n)\\)-bit descriptors contribute only a fixed polynomial in \\(\\lg n\\), hence \\(O(n)\\).</p><p>For division, form \\(a2^{-h}x\\) by an exact significand product and binary-point shift. Then\n\\[\n\\left|a2^{-h}x-\\frac ab\\right|\n\\le a2^{-h}2^{-P}&lt;2^{n-P}&lt;1/4.\n\\]\nIts floor \\(q_0\\) differs from \\(\\lfloor a/b\\rfloor\\) by at most one. Compute \\(r_0=a-bq_0\\). If \\(r_0&lt;0\\), decrement \\(q_0\\) and add \\(b\\); if \\(r_0\\ge b\\), increment \\(q_0\\) and subtract \\(b\\).</p><p>For square root, scaling the approximation by \\(2^e\\) gives error less than \\(2^{e-P}&lt;1/4\\). Its floor \\(s_0\\) differs from \\(\\lfloor\\sqrt a\\rfloor\\) by at most one. Decrement if \\(s_0^2&gt;a\\); increment if \\((s_0+1)^2\\le a\\); otherwise retain it.</p><p>All final test products have \\(O(n)\\) bits and use Theorem 1’s multiplier in \\(O(B(n))\\). Exact quotient and perfect-square cases are covered by the tests. Output padding is linear, and leading zeroes do not affect the bounds because \\(n\\) is the supplied width.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "the unnumbered matrix-transposition consequence in Section 1.7, proved in Section 11", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a deterministic fixed-finite-tape procedure which transposes the array, placing entry \\((i,j)\\) at position \\(n_1j+i\\), in \\( O\\!\\left(m(\\lg m)^{1-\\kappa}\\right) \\) time.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed-alphabet, fixed-finite-tape machine model and \\(\\kappa=2^{-182}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n_1,n_2,b\\) be positive integers.</p><p class=\"li\">A row-major \\(n_1\\times n_2\\) array of \\(b\\)-bit strings stores entry \\((i,j)\\) at position \\(n_2i+j\\), where \\(0\\le i&lt;n_1\\) and \\(0\\le j&lt;n_2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(m=n_1n_2b\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a deterministic fixed-finite-tape procedure which transposes the array, placing entry \\((i,j)\\) at position \\(n_1j+i\\), in\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(m(\\lg m)^{1-\\kappa}\\right)\n\\]\ntime.</p><p>In particular, an \\(n\\times n\\) binary matrix can be transposed in\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(n^2(\\lg n)^{1-\\kappa}\\right)\n\\]\ntime.</p>", "proof": "<p>The cited Harvey–van der Hoeven transposition reduction, reference [16], Corollary 6.2, converts a multiplication bound \\(M(m)\\) into a fixed-tape transposition bound\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(M(m)+m\\lg\\lg\\max(n_1,n_2)\\right),\n\\qquad n_1n_2b\\le m.\n\\]</p><p>Compute \\(m=n_1n_2b\\) from the binary descriptors. Their total length is \\(O(\\log(2m))\\); two schoolbook descriptor products cost \\(O(\\log^2(2m))=O(m)\\).</p><p>For every multiplication call of the cited interface, pad the supplied operands to exactly \\(m\\) bits, invoke Theorem 1 on their concatenation with \\(\\#\\), and normalize its \\(2m\\)-bit output to standard binary, using one zero digit for zero. Necessary orientation changes are linear tape reversals. These adaptations cost \\(O(m)\\) on fixed tapes.</p><p>Since \\(\\max(n_1,n_2)\\le m\\),\n\\[\n\\lg\\lg\\max(n_1,n_2)\n\\le\\lg\\lg m=o((\\lg m)^{1-\\kappa}).\n\\]\nThe reduction therefore costs \\(O(m(\\lg m)^{1-\\kappa})\\). Substituting \\(m=n_1n_2b\\) gives the rectangular result; for an \\(n\\times n\\) binary matrix, \\(m=n^2\\). Dimensions equal to one are covered by \\(\\lg1=1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Definition 22, “Grouped packed stream,” and Lemma 23, “Packed selected-bit addition for grouped rectangles.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "At the positions \\(j_i=\\rho+iK\\), numbered from the least significant bit, one fixed multitape procedure performs \\( y_{j_i}\\leftarrow y_{j_i}\\oplus x_{j_i} \\qquad(0\\le i<f) \\) in every group.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M,R\\ge1\\), \\(K\\ge6\\), \\(f\\ge1\\), \\(L=fK\\), and \\(0\\le\\rho&lt;K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix an ordered template of \\(t\\ge3\\) two-bit tags. The tag \\(00\\) denotes a spectator field, and \\(01,10,11\\) denote \\(x,y,z\\), respectively. Each chunk tag occurs exactly once.</p><p class=\"li\">The template is common to \\(J\\ge1\\) groups, but its length may vary between inputs.</p><p class=\"li\">Group \\(\\nu\\) has a positive row count \\(n_\\nu\\) and positive length \\(a_{\\nu,h}\\) at every spectator tag \\(h\\). At a chunk tag, put \\(a_{\\nu,h}=2^L\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  Q_\\nu=\\prod_{h=1}^t a_{\\nu,h},\\qquad\n  M_\\nu=n_\\nu Q_\\nu,\\qquad\n  C_\\nu=\\sum_{\\mu&lt;\\nu}M_\\mu,\\qquad\n  V_\\nu=M_\\nu R,\n  \\]\n  and require \\(M=\\sum_{\\nu=1}^J M_\\nu\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The payload stream consists, in group order, of the lexicographic rectangles\n  \\[\n  [n_\\nu]\\times\\prod_{h=1}^t[a_{\\nu,h}],\n  \\qquad [a]=\\{0,\\ldots,a-1\\},\n  \\]\n  with one \\(R\\)-bit record per address. Every row contains the full displayed Cartesian suffix.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(v\\ge1\\), put\n  \\[\n  b_v=\\lfloor\\log_2v\\rfloor+1,\\qquad\n  \\Gamma(v)=0^{b_v-1}\\operatorname{bin}_{b_v}(v).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(T_h\\) is the tag at position \\(h\\), the supplied descriptor is exactly\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D=\n  \\Gamma(M)\\Gamma(R)\\Gamma(K)\\Gamma(f)\\Gamma(\\rho+1)\\Gamma(t)\n  T_1\\cdots T_t\\Gamma(J)\n  \\prod_{\\nu=1}^J\n  \\left(\\Gamma(n_\\nu)\\prod_{h:T_h=00}\\Gamma(a_{\\nu,h})\\right),\n  \\]\n  where products mean concatenation in increasing index order.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that the descriptor and accompanying records are valid with these parameters, and that \\(A\\ge2\\) bounds the actual total bit length of \\(\\mathcal D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;\\tau&lt;1\\) be the fixed address-interchange exponent constructed in Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At the positions \\(j_i=\\rho+iK\\), numbered from the least significant bit, one fixed multitape procedure performs\n\\[\ny_{j_i}\\leftarrow y_{j_i}\\oplus x_{j_i}\n\\qquad(0\\le i&lt;f)\n\\]\nin every group. It fixes the group, row ordinal, spectator coordinates, all other chunk bits, and every record payload; in particular, it restores \\(z\\) for every initial value.</p><p>For \\(V=MR\\), its time is\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(\nV(L^\\tau+1)+MA^3+\nM\\min\\{1,80(f-1)2^{-K}\\}\\,A(R+A)\n\\right).\n\\]\nWhen \\(A=O(p)\\), \\(R\\) exceeds every fixed polynomial in \\(p\\), \\(f\\le d\\le p\\), and \\(K/\\log p\\to\\infty\\), this is\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(V(L^\\tau+1)\\right),\n\\]\nuniformly over the permitted group counts, shapes, and payloads.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(s=t-3\\) be the number of spectator tags. Each group stores \\(s+1\\) positive integer codes, so\n\\[\nJ(s+1)\\le A,\\qquad Jt=J(s+3)\\le3A.\n\\]\nAlso \\(J\\le M\\), and the header implies\n\\[\n\\log_2M+\\log_2R=O(A).\n\\]\nAll field lengths, partial products, row counts, group counts, and volumes have \\(O(A)\\)-bit descriptions. Hence\n\\[\nA_\\nu:=\\lceil\\log_2(2V_\\nu)\\rceil=O(A)\n\\]\nuniformly over groups.</p><p>Parse the descriptor in forward group order, keeping the common template separately. In each group, collapse the row ordinal and spectators before the first chunk into a prefix, and collapse each spectator interval between and after chunks. Empty intervals have length one. This produces the four positive lengths required by Proposition 4.</p><p>Fixing a collapsed coordinate fixes every mixed-radix factor, so Proposition 4 preserves the original row ordinal and spectator coordinates. Forming the local descriptor costs \\(O(A^3)\\) per group: there are \\(O(A)\\) field visits and schoolbook operations on \\(O(A)\\)-bit integers. The local routine receives only its fixed number of collapsed lengths, \\(R,K,f,\\rho\\), and the finite chunk-name permutation; it does not rescan the group table.</p><p>Use forward outer input and output cursors. Copy one group into a local area, perform Proposition 4’s complete rectangular algorithm, append its output, and clear the local region. Reuse the same tapes for every group. No local call scans an earlier or later group. Copying, appending, positioning, cleanup, and the final rewind total \\(O(V)\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\delta_{\\rm bad}=\\min\\{1,80(f-1)2^{-K}\\}.\n\\]\nFor group \\(\\nu\\), Proposition 4 gives\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(\nV_\\nu(L^\\tau+1)+M_\\nu A_\\nu^3+\n\\delta_{\\rm bad}M_\\nu A_\\nu(R+A_\\nu)\n\\right).\n\\]\nExceptional extraction, sorting, and reinsertion remain within that group; only local rank keys are required.</p><p>Sum over groups, use\n\\[\n\\sum_\\nu M_\\nu=M,\\qquad\n\\sum_\\nu V_\\nu=V,\\qquad A_\\nu=O(A),\\qquad J\\le M,\n\\]\nand include descriptor work. The result is\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(\nV(L^\\tau+1)+MA^3+\\delta_{\\rm bad}MA(R+A)\n\\right).\n\\]\nThis counts the complete record payload in every exceptional-sort pass. The case \\(f=1\\) uses Proposition 4’s direct final XOR, with no rotations or repair.</p><p>After division by \\(V=MR\\), the extra terms are bounded by\n\\[\nA^3/R,\\qquad80(f-1)2^{-K}A(1+A/R).\n\\]\nUnder the stated family assumptions, the first tends to zero and the second is\n\\[\nO(p^2\\,2^{-K}(1+A/R))=o(1).\n\\]\nThe bounds depend on the supplied groups only through \\(M,R,A\\), proving uniformity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Section 2 and Lemmas 3–4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The elementary digit movements, aligned pointwise stream operations, cyclic row distribution, controlled shifts, and fixed rational coordinate scalings specified in Lemmas 1.1–1.2 have linear bit cost on fixed tapes.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Arrays on \\([a_1]\\times\\cdots\\times[a_t]\\) are stored lexicographically, with the first coordinate most significant.</p><p class=\"li\">Addresses are implicit in stream position. A trailing \\(B\\)-bit record may be regarded as a final address field \\([B]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Logical volume \\(V\\) counts stored data bits, not duplicate streams or work tapes.</p><p class=\"li\">An address permutation \\(f\\) moves the entry at \\(x\\) to \\(f(x)\\), so\n  \\[\n  (fA)(f(x))=A(x).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Irrelevant adjacent fields may be collapsed to their product. Elementary calls receive a fixed number of canonical binary lengths.</p><p class=\"li\">All fields are nonempty; empty arrays are handled directly.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The elementary digit movements, aligned pointwise stream operations, cyclic row distribution, controlled shifts, and fixed rational coordinate scalings specified in Lemmas 1.1–1.2 have linear bit cost on fixed tapes. Their costs include descriptor preparation, counters, head returns, and cleanup.</p><p>Fixed-width signed fixed-point addition, subtraction, sign change, and multiplication or division by a fixed power of two are also linear-cost streaming operations when sufficient exact guard bits are allocated.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 1.1–1.2 establish the address and stream operations.</p><p>For signed fixed-point arithmetic, reverse a most-significant-bit-first record onto an arithmetic work tape when necessary. With least significant bits first, propagate carries in finite control, then reverse the completed record if its external order requires it. Sufficient integer and fractional guard bits make the stated operations exact. Each reversal and arithmetic pass has linear bit cost.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 3, “Elementary stream operations.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Each of the following operations on volume \\(V\\) costs \\(O(V)\\):", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 1’s array conventions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a radix \\(q\\ge2\\), a pointwise map, and a positive integer \\(W\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each of the following operations on volume \\(V\\) costs \\(O(V)\\):</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Any fixed permutation of the pair of digit values at two specified radix-\\(q\\) positions, adjacent or not.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Moving one specified radix-\\(q\\) digit to another position while preserving the intervening order.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Splitting on one specified radix-\\(q\\) digit, applying the fixed pointwise map to aligned streams, and merging.</p><p class=\"li num\">4. For an array on \\([P]\\times[R]\\times[B]\\), distributing whole rows cyclically into \\(W\\) streams within each prefix; and, when \\(W\\mid R\\), merging them in any fixed order of row classes.</p><p>The constants may depend on \\(q\\), the fixed map, and \\(W\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first operation, split into \\(q^2\\) streams according to the two specified digits. With those digits fixed, the order of the remaining digits is unchanged. Enumerate output addresses in their required order and read the stream prescribed by the two preimage digits.</p><p>A digit move uses \\(q\\) streams. Splitting on one digit likewise produces streams with the same remaining address set and order; scan aligned entries, apply the fixed map, and merge.</p><p>For rows, write \\(r=Wg+w\\), \\(0\\le w&lt;W\\), and send the whole row to stream \\(w\\). When \\(W\\mid R\\), every stream has address set\n\\[\n[P]\\times[R/W]\\times[B]\n\\]\nand volume \\(V/W\\). The inverse merge reads the classes in the specified order.</p><p>A binary counter is kept with its head at the least significant end. Over \\(L\\) consecutive increments or decrements, returning that head costs \\(O(L+w)\\) for width \\(w\\), since position \\(j\\) is visited \\(O(1+L/2^j)\\) times. Initialization absorbs the width term. Store a local copy of each range length, so resets do not search larger descriptors. Induction over a fixed number of nonempty nested ranges gives linear total counting cost.</p><p>Preparing the fixed number of collapsed lengths costs\n\\[\nO((\\log(2V))^C)=O(V)\n\\]\nfor a fixed \\(C\\). Every stream is scanned a fixed number of times, and rewinds, copies, and erasures cover only already charged regions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 4, “Controlled shifts and fixed rational scalings.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The update \\( y\\leftarrow y+a(\\text{controls})\\pmod Q \\) costs \\(O(V)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a prime \\(q\\), a fixed number of control fields, and finitely many rational coefficients with denominators prime to \\(q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Coefficients used as units also have numerators prime to \\(q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(Q=q^b\\), \\(b\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A target field \\(y\\in[Q]\\) follows its controls; every control used in an offset has \\(O(b)\\) digits.</p><p class=\"li\">The offset \\(a(\\text{controls})\\) can be prepared in \\(b^{O(1)}\\) time.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The update\n\\[\ny\\leftarrow y+a(\\text{controls})\\pmod Q\n\\]\ncosts \\(O(V)\\). This includes fixed rational linear combinations specialized modulo \\(Q\\).</p><p>Multiplication of \\(y\\) by any specified rational unit, and its inverse, also costs \\(O(V)\\). Arbitrarily long intervening spectators and suffix records are allowed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix all fields before the target and let the suffix contain \\(B\\) bits. A fiber consists of \\(Q\\) consecutive \\(B\\)-bit blocks. For offset \\(a\\in[Q]\\), split at \\(Q-a\\), append the two pieces to two tapes, and merge the second piece before the first. Recompute \\(a\\) at the beginning of the corresponding merge fiber.</p><p>The \\(b^{O(1)}\\) offset and reset work is absorbed by the fiber because, for every fixed \\(C\\),\n\\[\nb^C=O(q^b)=O(Q),\\qquad QB\\ge Q.\n\\]\nSpectator counters are incremented by ripple counting and are not fully consulted to compute the offset.</p><p>For a positive fixed integer \\(c\\) prime to \\(q\\), the map \\(y\\mapsto cy\\bmod Q\\) has \\(c\\) increasing pieces, classified by\n\\[\nj=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{cy}{Q}\\right\\rfloor,\\qquad 0\\le j&lt;c.\n\\]\nPrepare the endpoints, split into these streams, and enumerate \\(y'=0,\\ldots,Q-1\\). The unique \\(j\\in[c]\\) satisfying\n\\[\ny'+jQ\\equiv0\\pmod c\n\\]\nselects the next stream, with \\(y=(y'+jQ)/c\\). A finite residue counter maintains this choice. Reversing split and merge implements \\(c^{-1}\\).</p><p>Negation fixes the zero block and reverses the remaining \\(Q-1\\) blocks. Push them to a temporary tape, pop them in reverse order, and reverse each popped block on another tape to restore its internal bit order. Every bit moves a bounded number of times. Signed rational units are compositions of these operations.</p><p>The nested scan analysis of Lemma 1.1 applies. In particular, copying a suffix-length counter at a target position costs \\(O(\\log(2B))\\le O(B)\\), not \\(O(\\log V)\\) per bit. Reductions of fixed denominators modulo \\(q^b\\) are prepared by ordinary extended Euclidean arithmetic in polynomial time in \\(b\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Propositions 7–8, with the scalar and subspace constructions of Lemmas 5–6 and the bounds in Section 3.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are two fixed networks with the following interfaces.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  h=100,\\quad v=\\binom h3=161700,\\quad\n  N=v^3=4227952113000000,\\quad m=h^3=1000000,\\quad\n  I=3v^2=78440670000.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal T\\) be the three-element subsets of \\([h]\\). Data roles are \\(X_a,Y_a\\), \\(a\\in\\mathcal T^3\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The bit network has scalar ring \\(\\mathbb F_2\\). The complex network has scalar ring \\(\\mathbb Z[i,1/2]\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A role is a fixed wire position. Its vertices are its source, successive gates touching it, and sink. An edge joins consecutive vertices.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\varrho\\) exchange \\(X_a,Y_a\\) and fix all auxiliary roles.</p><p class=\"li\">The bit label space is\n  \\[\n  F_b=\\mathbb Q^h,\\qquad\n  \\langle x,y\\rangle=x^{\\mathsf T}(I-J/9)y,\n  \\qquad \\mathcal F_b=F_b^{\\otimes3}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The complex label space is\n  \\[\n  F_c=\\mathbb F_2^h\n  \\]\n  with ordinary dot product, and \\(\\mathcal F_c=F_c^{\\otimes3}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a triple \\(T\\), \\(t_T\\) is its indicator vector. For \\(a=(a_1,a_2,a_3)\\), put\n  \\[\n  u_a=t_{a_1}\\otimes t_{a_2}\\otimes t_{a_3},\\qquad U_a=\\langle u_a\\rangle.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Every auxiliary scalar may initially be arbitrary.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are two fixed networks with the following interfaces.</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Rational matrix interface, original Proposition 7:\n   \\[\n   W_b=177176569091445000000.\n   \\]\n   The bit network uses pointwise XOR gates and implements \\(\\varrho\\). Fixed rational matrices \\(M_\\xi\\), common to all incidences of each gate \\(\\xi\\), satisfy\n   \\[\n   M_{\\operatorname{sink}(\\varrho(w))}\n   -M_{\\operatorname{source}(w)}=I_m\n   \\]\n   for every role \\(w\\), and\n   \\[\n   s_b=\\sum_e\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb Q}\n   (M_{\\operatorname{head}(e)}-M_{\\operatorname{tail}(e)})\n   =W_bm-N+2L_b\n   =177176569088785861287000000&lt;W_bm,\n   \\]\n   where \\(L_b=I\\cdot100\\cdot h\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Binary phase interface, original Proposition 8:\n   \\[\n   W_c=1873807244643542670000.\n   \\]\n   For a binary label \\(U\\), let \\(P_U\\) be orthogonal projection and put\n   \\[\n   q_U(x)=\\operatorname{wt}(P_Ux)\\pmod4,\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix},\n   \\qquad\n   C_U=H^{\\otimes m}\\operatorname{diag}_x(i^{q_U(x)})H^{\\otimes m}.\n   \\]\n   Write\n   \\[\n   C=H\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)H=aI+bX,\\qquad\n   a=\\frac{1+i}{2},\\quad b=\\frac{1-i}{2},\n   \\]\n   where \\(X\\) flips one bit. Each edge frame change \\(C_VC_U^{-1}\\) is one forward or inverse kernel \\(aI+bX_z\\) per vector \\(z\\) in an orthonormal basis of its orthogonal residual, where\n   \\[\n   (X_zf)(x)=f(x+z).\n   \\]\n   The total basis length is\n   \\[\n   s_c=W_cm-2N+2L_c\n   =1873807244636671267308000000&lt;W_cm,\n   \\]\n   where \\(L_c=I\\cdot101\\cdot h\\).</p><p>   After the specified source and sink diagonal corrections, sign corrections, and undoing the bank exchange, the endpoint operator is \\(C^{\\otimes m}\\) on every role, including auxiliary roles. Tensoring over any positive number of columns preserves this assertion.</p><p>For both interfaces,\n\\[\n\\tau=\\sigma=1-2^{-50}\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\ns_b/W_b&lt;m^\\tau,\\qquad s_c/W_c&lt;m^\\sigma.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The neighbor counts are\n\\[\nz_b=3\\binom{97}{2}=13968,\\qquad\nz_c=\\binom{97}{3}+3\\cdot97=147731.\n\\]\nWith \\(c_b=100,c_c=101\\),\n\\[\nW_b=2N+I(vz_b+c_b),\\qquad\nW_c=2N+I(vz_c+c_c).\n\\]\nOrdered neighboring pairs give \\(vz\\) side wires per invocation.</p><p>For arrays on a common address set \\(\\Omega\\), assign an invertible scalar-linear frame \\(D_\\xi\\) to every source, gate, and sink. On an edge \\(\\xi\\to\\zeta\\), apply \\(D_\\zeta D_\\xi^{-1}\\). All incidences of a gate use its common frame, so\n\\[\nG(D_\\xi g_1,\\ldots,D_\\xi g_t)=D_\\xi G(g_1,\\ldots,g_t).\n\\]\nFor physical input \\(f\\), interpret the logical input as \\(D_{\\rm in}^{-1}f\\), without physical preprocessing. The implemented operator is\n\\[\nD_{\\rm out}S_\\Omega D_{\\rm in}^{-1}.\n\\]\nA signed role route \\(w\\to\\varrho(w)\\) therefore applies its sign times\n\\[\nD_{\\operatorname{sink}(\\varrho(w))}\nD_{\\operatorname{source}(w)}^{-1}.\n\\]</p><p>For the rational interface, assign \\(M_\\xi=P_U\\) at a gate or sink labeled \\(U\\), assign \\(-P_{U_a}\\) at source \\(X_a\\), and zero at every other source. For comparable nondegenerate labels, the projection difference has rank equal to the dimension change. The \\(N\\) negative source projections add rank one each: the first \\(X_a\\) edge is \\(2P_{U_a}\\), whereas its label dimension change is zero. Lemma 2.2 gives\n\\[\ns_b=W_bm-N+2L_b.\n\\]\nThe endpoint difference on \\(X_a\\to Y_a\\) is\n\\[\nP_{U_a^\\perp}-(-P_{U_a})=I_m;\n\\]\nall other routes have zero source matrix and identity sink matrix. Lemma 2.1 supplies the scalar permutation.</p><p>For the phase interface, if \\(V=U\\perp E\\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\) is an orthonormal residual basis, then orthogonality and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{wt}(z+w)=\\operatorname{wt}(z)+\\operatorname{wt}(w)\n-2|\\operatorname{supp}(z)\\cap\\operatorname{supp}(w)|\n\\]\ngive\n\\[\nq_V(x)-q_U(x)=\n\\sum_{z\\in\\mathcal B}\\operatorname{wt}(z)[z\\cdot x]\\pmod4.\n\\]\nNegate this expression for decreasing edges. Every basis vector has odd weight. For \\(\\epsilon\\in\\{1,-1\\}\\),\n\\[\ni^{\\epsilon[z\\cdot x]}\n=\\frac{1+i^\\epsilon}{2}\n+\\frac{1-i^\\epsilon}{2}(-1)^{z\\cdot x}.\n\\]\nHadamard conjugation sends the character to translation \\(X_z\\), giving one \\(aI+bX_z\\), or its inverse, per basis vector. Lemma 2.2 gives \\(s_c\\). All frame entries and inverses are Gaussian dyadics.</p><p>Zero and full labels have frames \\(I\\) and \\(C^{\\otimes m}\\). All routes except \\(X_a\\to Y_a\\) already have these endpoints. For that route, \\(\\operatorname{wt}(u_a)=27\\), and\n\\[\nq_{U_a^\\perp}(x)-q_{U_a}(x)\n=\\operatorname{wt}(x)+2[u_a\\cdot x]\\pmod4.\n\\]\nThus its endpoint is \\(C^{-1}\\) on the 27 support coordinates and \\(C\\) elsewhere. With \\(Z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\\),\n\\[\nC=iZC^{-1}Z,\\qquad C^{-1}=-iZCZ.\n\\]\nFor \\(k\\) columns, apply the support sign \\(Z_{a,k}\\) at source \\(X_a\\), then \\(i^{27k}Z_{a,k}\\) at sink \\(Y_a\\). Negate the physical \\(X\\) outputs and undo the bank exchange. Every role then receives \\(C^{\\otimes mk}\\).</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\nL_b/N=100/539,\\qquad L_c/N=101/539.\n\\]\nThe relative deficits are\n\\[\n\\eta_b=\\frac{339}{22587335000000},\\qquad\n\\eta_c=\\frac{73}{19906842167500},\n\\]\nboth greater than \\(20\\cdot2^{-50}\\). Since \\(m&lt;2^{20}\\) and \\(\\log m&lt;20\\),\n\\[\nm^{1-2^{-50}}\n=m\\exp(-2^{-50}\\log m)\n&gt;m(1-20\\cdot2^{-50})&gt;m(1-\\eta).\n\\]\nThis proves the stated recurrence exponents.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A forward invocation sends \\(y_S\\) to \\(y_S+x_S\\), leaving every other value unchanged, independently of all initial auxiliary values.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 2’s data roles.</p><p class=\"li\">At stage \\(j\\in\\{1,2,3\\}\\), fix the other two triple coordinates and vary coordinate \\(j\\), giving \\(v^2\\) invocations.</p><p class=\"li\">Every invocation has distinct auxiliary wires.</p><p class=\"li\">In a forward invocation, controls are \\(x_T\\), targets are \\(y_S\\), \\(S,T\\in\\mathcal T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In the bit construction, neighbors satisfy \\(|S\\cap T|=1\\). In the complex construction, neighbors are distinct triples with even intersection.</p><p class=\"li\">There is a side wire \\(A_{ST}\\) for every ordered neighboring pair and central wires \\(C_i\\), \\(i\\in[h]\\); the complex construction also has \\(C_*\\).</p><p>The maps are:</p><p class=\"li\">Copy: \\(A_{ST}\\leftarrow A_{ST}+x_T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Bit side injection: \\(y_S\\leftarrow y_S+\\sum_{T:|S\\cap T|=1}A_{ST}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Bit gather: \\(C_i\\leftarrow C_i+\\sum_{T\\ni i}x_T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Bit scatter: \\(y_S\\leftarrow y_S+\\sum_{i\\in S}C_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Complex side injection:\n  \\[\n  y_S\\leftarrow y_S-\\sum_{T\\text{ neighboring }S}\n  \\frac{|S\\cap T|-1}{2}A_{ST}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Complex gather also adds \\(\\sum_Tx_T\\) to \\(C_*\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Complex scatter adds \\((\\sum_{i\\in S}C_i-C_*)/2\\) to \\(y_S\\).</p><p>The forward schedule is:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|l|l}\n0&amp;\\text{subtract side injection}&amp;\\text{one gate per target}\\\\\n1&amp;\\text{subtract scatter}&amp;\\text{one gate on targets and center}\\\\\n2&amp;\\text{copy}&amp;\\text{one gate per source}\\\\\n3&amp;\\text{gather}&amp;\\text{one gate on sources and center}\\\\\n4&amp;\\text{scatter}&amp;\\text{one gate on targets and center}\\\\\n5&amp;\\text{side injection}&amp;\\text{one gate per target}\\\\\n6&amp;\\text{undo gather}&amp;\\text{one gate on sources and center}\\\\\n7&amp;\\text{undo copy}&amp;\\text{one gate per source}.\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A forward invocation sends \\(y_S\\) to \\(y_S+x_S\\), leaving every other value unchanged, independently of all initial auxiliary values.</p><p>A forward stage \\(X\\to Y\\), an inverse forward stage \\(Y\\to X\\), and a forward stage \\(X\\to Y\\) send\n\\[\n(X_a,Y_a)\\longmapsto(-Y_a,X_a)\n\\]\nand restore every auxiliary value. Over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\), this is the bank exchange.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(V,G,J,R\\) for copy, gather, side injection, and scatter. If the initial side and center vectors are \\(a,c\\), the first two rows subtract \\(Ja+Rc\\). Rows 2–3 change the auxiliary vectors to \\(a+Vx,c+Gx\\). Rows 4–5 add\n\\[\nR(c+Gx)+J(a+Vx).\n\\]\nRows 6–7 restore \\(c,a\\). The target change is therefore\n\\[\n(JV+RG)x.\n\\]</p><p>In the bit case, the central coefficient is \\(|S\\cap T|\\bmod2\\). The side contribution cancels intersection-one cases; distinct triples with intersection zero or two already have coefficient zero, and the self coefficient is one.</p><p>In the complex case, the central coefficient is \\((|S\\cap T|-1)/2\\). The side cancels it for distinct triples with intersection zero or two; intersection one contributes zero, and the self coefficient is one.</p><p>Thus the net map is \\(y\\leftarrow y+x\\). The inverse schedule gives \\(X\\leftarrow X-Y\\). Starting from \\((x,y)\\), the three stages give\n\\[\n(x,y+x),\\qquad(-y,y+x),\\qquad(-y,x).\n\\]\nEach invocation restores its own auxiliary wires.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every edge joins comparable nondegenerate labels.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 2’s label spaces and Lemma 2.1’s network.</p><p class=\"li\">Sources and sinks have labels\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|cc}\n  \\text{role}&amp;\\text{source}&amp;\\text{sink}\\\\ \\hline\n  X_a&amp;U_a&amp;\\mathcal F\\\\\n  Y_a&amp;0&amp;U_a^\\perp\\\\\n  \\text{auxiliary}&amp;0&amp;\\mathcal F.\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">At stage \\(j\\), for one invocation, put\n  \\[\n  A=F^{\\otimes(j-1)},\\quad\n  P=\\langle t_{a_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes t_{a_{j-1}}\\rangle,\\quad\n  B=P^\\perp\\subset A,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  Q=\\langle t_{a_{j+1}}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes t_{a_3}\\rangle.\n  \\]\n  Empty tensor products are the one-dimensional ground field.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppressing the factor \\(Q\\), assign the successive gate labels\n  \\[\n  \\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n  \\text{time}&amp;\\text{physical wires}&amp;\\text{label}\\\\ \\hline\n  0&amp;Y,\\text{ side wires}&amp;B\\otimes\\langle t\\rangle\\\\\n  1&amp;Y,\\text{ center}&amp;B\\otimes F\\\\\n  2&amp;X,\\text{ side wires}&amp;(B\\otimes F)\\perp(P\\otimes\\langle t\\rangle)\\\\\n  3&amp;X,\\text{ center}&amp;A\\otimes F\\\\\n  4&amp;Y,\\text{ center}&amp;B\\otimes F\\\\\n  5&amp;Y,\\text{ side wires}&amp;(B\\otimes F)\\perp(P\\otimes t^\\perp)\\\\\n  6&amp;X,\\text{ center}&amp;A\\otimes F\\\\\n  7&amp;X,\\text{ side wires}&amp;A\\otimes F.\n  \\end{array}\n  \\]\n  Every label is tensored by \\(Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At stages 1 and 3, time \\(r\\) uses row \\(r\\) of the forward schedule. At stage 2, it uses the inverse of row \\(7-r\\), with logical banks exchanged.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every edge joins comparable nondegenerate labels. The only decreasing edges are center-wire edges from time 3 to time 4, each losing dimension \\(h\\).</p><p>If \\(c\\) is the number of central wires per invocation and \\(W\\) the total number of wires, then\n\\[\nL=Ich,\\qquad\n\\sum_e\\left|\\dim U_{\\operatorname{head}(e)}\n-\\dim U_{\\operatorname{tail}(e)}\\right|\n=Wm-2N+2L.\n\\]\nFor the binary label space, every nonzero orthogonal residual has an orthonormal basis.</p>", "proof": "<p>The rational form has eigenvalues \\(1\\) and \\(1-h/9=-91/9\\). For triple indicators,\n\\[\n\\langle t_S,t_T\\rangle=|S\\cap T|-1,\\qquad\n\\langle t_T,t_T\\rangle=2.\n\\]\nIn the binary space, \\(t_T\\cdot t_T=1\\). Thus triple lines are nondegenerate and neighboring lines are orthogonal in the respective constructions.</p><p>The prescribed stage transitions are\n\\[\nX:\\ A\\otimes\\langle t\\rangle\\otimes Q\n\\longrightarrow(A\\otimes F)\\otimes Q,\n\\]\n\\[\nY:\\ B\\otimes\\langle t\\rangle\\otimes Q\n\\longrightarrow\n\\bigl((B\\otimes F)\\perp(P\\otimes t^\\perp)\\bigr)\\otimes Q.\n\\]\nFor \\(j&lt;3\\), writing \\(Q=\\langle t_{a_{j+1}}\\rangle\\otimes Q'\\), the next stage has\n\\[\nA'=A\\otimes F,\\quad P'=P\\otimes\\langle t\\rangle,\\quad\nB'=(P')^\\perp=(B\\otimes F)\\perp(P\\otimes t^\\perp).\n\\]\nHence the transitions fit at stage boundaries and match the terminal labels.</p><p>Put \\(a=\\dim A=h^{j-1}\\), \\(f=h^{3-j}\\). The residuals, tensored by \\(Q\\) except in the last row, are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{edge}&amp;\\text{residual}&amp;\\text{dimension}\\\\ \\hline\nX:\\mathrm{in}\\to2&amp;B\\otimes t_X^\\perp&amp;(a-1)(h-1)\\\\\nX:2\\to3&amp;P\\otimes t_X^\\perp&amp;h-1\\\\\nX:3\\to6,\\ 6\\to7&amp;0&amp;0\\\\\nY:\\mathrm{in}\\to0&amp;0&amp;0\\\\\nY:0\\to1&amp;B\\otimes t_Y^\\perp&amp;(a-1)(h-1)\\\\\nY:1\\to4&amp;0&amp;0\\\\\nY:4\\to5&amp;P\\otimes t_Y^\\perp&amp;h-1\\\\\n\\mathrm{center}:\\mathrm{source}\\to1&amp;B\\otimes F&amp;(a-1)h\\\\\n\\mathrm{center}:1\\to3&amp;P\\otimes F&amp;h\\\\\n\\mathrm{center}:3\\to4&amp;P\\otimes F&amp;h\\\\\n\\mathrm{center}:4\\to6&amp;P\\otimes F&amp;h\\\\\n\\mathrm{side}:\\mathrm{source}\\to0&amp;B\\otimes\\langle t_Y\\rangle&amp;a-1\\\\\n\\mathrm{side}:0\\to2&amp;\n(B\\otimes t_Y^\\perp)\\perp(P\\otimes\\langle t_X\\rangle)&amp;\n(a-1)(h-1)+1\\\\\n\\mathrm{side}:2\\to5&amp;P\\otimes\\langle t_X,t_Y\\rangle^\\perp&amp;h-2\\\\\n\\mathrm{side}:5\\to7&amp;P\\otimes\\langle t_Y\\rangle&amp;1\\\\\n\\mathrm{auxiliary}:\\mathrm{last}\\to\\mathrm{sink}&amp;\n(A\\otimes F)\\otimes Q^\\perp&amp;ah(f-1).\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe final data edges have zero residual.</p><p>For side edge \\(2\\to5\\), orthogonality gives\n\\[\n\\langle t_X\\rangle\\subset t_Y^\\perp,\n\\]\nso its labels are nested; the displayed residual is the orthogonal complement. Every other entry follows by splitting\n\\[\nA=B\\perp P,\\qquad F=\\langle t\\rangle\\perp t^\\perp.\n\\]\nOnly center edge \\(3\\to4\\) decreases.</p><p>The signed dimension sum telescopes at gates. Source dimensions sum to \\(N\\), and sink dimensions to \\(Wm-N\\), giving signed increase \\(Wm-2N\\). Replacing signed changes by absolute changes adds \\(2L\\).</p><p>Over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\), every nonzero residual contains a norm-one vector. The lines \\(P,Q\\) do; when nonzero, \\(B\\) and \\(Q^\\perp\\) contain coordinate units outside supports of sizes \\(3^{j-1}\\) and \\(3^{3-j}\\). Triple complements and complements of two neighboring triple lines contain coordinate units outside supports of size at most three and six. Tensoring gives norm-one vectors in the nonzero summands.</p><p>A nondegenerate nonalternating binary symmetric space has an orthonormal basis: split off unit lines until the remainder is alternating; split that remainder into planes with \\(a\\cdot a=b\\cdot b=0\\), \\(a\\cdot b=1\\). A plane orthogonal to a unit vector \\(w\\) is absorbed using the three independent pairwise orthogonal norm-one vectors\n\\[\nw+a,\\qquad w+b,\\qquad w+a+b.\n\\]\nRepeating absorbs every alternating plane.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Lemma 12, “Arbitrary-width interchange,” together with Section 4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One fixed-alphabet, fixed-finite-tape procedure implements \\( (p,h,g,d,z)\\longmapsto(p,d,g,h,z) \\) in \\(O(Vu^\\tau)\\) time.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 1’s stream conventions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P,G,B\\) be positive integers and \\(u\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The arbitrary input bit array has address set\n  \\[\n  [P]\\times[2^u]\\times[G]\\times[2^u]\\times[B]\n  \\]\n  and volume \\(V=PGB\\,2^{2u}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Shape parameters are supplied in canonical binary.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\tau=1-2^{-50}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One fixed-alphabet, fixed-finite-tape procedure implements\n\\[\n(p,h,g,d,z)\\longmapsto(p,d,g,h,z)\n\\]\nin \\(O(Vu^\\tau)\\) time. This includes shape processing, padding and unpadding, and workspace cleanup, including when \\(B=1\\).</p><p>Address chunks whose widths differ by one bit can also be interchanged using one equal-width interchange and one single-bit move.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 3.3 to Proposition 2’s bit network.</p><p>First consider radix-\\(q\\) chunks of width \\(e\\). Put\n\\[\nk=\\lceil\\log_m e\\rceil,\\qquad\n\\rho=\\left\\lceil\\frac{k\\log W}{2\\log q}\\right\\rceil.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(e\\), \\(1\\le\\rho&lt;e\\) and \\(\\rho=O(\\log(2e))\\). Bounded smaller widths are handled digit by digit.</p><p>Write each chunk as high and low parts \\(h^+,h^-\\) and \\(d^+,d^-\\), with high width \\(\\rho\\). Exchange corresponding high digits, then move \\(h^+\\) next to \\(d^+\\). Suppressing outer fields, the successive orders are\n\\[\n(h^+,h^-,g,d^+,d^-),\n\\quad(d^+,h^+,h^-,g,d^-),\n\\]\n\\[\n(d^+,h^+,d^-,g,h^-),\n\\quad(d^+,d^-,g,h^+,h^-).\n\\]\nThe joined high parts supply a preceding row field of range\n\\[\nR=q^{2\\rho}\\ge W^k.\n\\]\nThe initial digit exchanges and joining cost \\(O(V\\rho)\\). Pad this row field within each prefix to\n\\[\nR'=W^k\\lceil R/W^k\\rceil&lt;2R.\n\\]</p><p>Expand\n\\[\ne-\\rho=\\sum_{j=0}^k a_jm^j,\\qquad0\\le a_j&lt;m.\n\\]\nPartition corresponding low parts into these pieces and apply Lemma 3.3 to each pair. The same row field is divisible by every required \\(W^j\\). Completed calls preserve row addresses, so genuine and padded rows do not mix. Their total cost is\n\\[\nO(V)\\sum_j a_jm^{j\\tau}=O(Ve^\\tau).\n\\]\nRemove padded rows and undo only the joining moves. The remaining cost \\(O(V\\log(2e))\\) is \\(O(Ve^\\tau)\\). Descriptor work over the \\(O(\\log(2e))\\) pieces is \\(O(V)\\).</p><p>For binary width \\(u\\), choose the least \\(e\\) with \\(q^e\\ge2^u\\). Numerically pad each target range from \\([2^u]\\) to \\([q^e]\\), with zeros outside the original Cartesian ranges. The volume increases by less than \\(q^2\\). Numerical lexicographic order is unchanged; no address-by-address radix conversion is required. The completed interchange preserves the valid set. Remove invalid entries. Since \\(e=O(u)\\), the cost is \\(O(Vu^\\tau)\\).</p><p>If the first chunk is one bit longer, write it \\((h_0,H)\\) and use\n\\[\n(h_0,H,g,D)\\to(h_0,D,g,H)\\to(D,g,h_0,H).\n\\]\nIf the second is longer, write it \\((d_0,D)\\) and use\n\\[\n(H,g,d_0,D)\\to(d_0,H,g,D)\\to(d_0,D,g,H).\n\\]\nEach uses one equal-width interchange and one linear-cost single-bit move.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 9, “Lower triangular factorization.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are invertible lower triangular rational matrices \\(E_1,E_2\\) and a matrix \\(\\Pi\\) with exactly \\(a\\) entries equal to one, at most one in each row and column, and all other entries zero, such that …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\) be a rational \\(m\\times m\\) matrix of rank \\(a\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are invertible lower triangular rational matrices \\(E_1,E_2\\) and a matrix \\(\\Pi\\) with exactly \\(a\\) entries equal to one, at most one in each row and column, and all other entries zero, such that\n\\[\nA=E_1\\Pi E_2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(A=0\\), take \\(E_1=E_2=I,\\Pi=0\\). Otherwise choose the topmost nonzero row and its rightmost nonzero entry. Use the pivot column to clear entries to its left by right multiplication by lower triangular elementary matrices. Clear entries below by left multiplication by lower triangular elementary matrices, and scale the pivot to one.</p><p>Delete the pivot row and column from the active index sets, retaining their original orders, and repeat. Operations on active indices remain lower triangular and do not disturb earlier isolated pivots. Each step isolates a rank-one block, so the final partial permutation has \\(a\\) ones.</p><p>If \\(LAR=\\Pi\\), then \\(E_1=L^{-1}\\), \\(E_2=R^{-1}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 10, “The cost of a rational matrix shear.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A fixed odd prime \\(q\\) can be chosen so that, for \\(A\\in\\mathcal A\\) of rational rank \\(a\\), \\( (H,D)\\longmapsto(H+AD,D) \\quad\\text{over }\\mathbb Z/q^b\\mathbb Z \\) uses exactly \\(a\\) interchanges of pairs of …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be a fixed finite collection of rational \\(m\\times m\\) matrices.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H=(H_1,\\ldots,H_m)\\), \\(D=(D_1,\\ldots,D_m)\\), each field ranging over \\([q^b]\\), with every \\(H_i\\) preceding every \\(D_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Spectators may lie between formal fields.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A fixed odd prime \\(q\\) can be chosen so that, for \\(A\\in\\mathcal A\\) of rational rank \\(a\\),\n\\[\n(H,D)\\longmapsto(H+AD,D)\n\\quad\\text{over }\\mathbb Z/q^b\\mathbb Z\n\\]\nuses exactly \\(a\\) interchanges of pairs of \\(b\\)-digit fields, each between an \\(H\\) and a \\(D\\) field, and \\(O(V)\\) additional work.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose Lemma 3.1’s factorizations for every matrix once and for all, including factors and inverses in a fixed rational table. Choose an odd prime avoiding all denominators and all numerators of diagonal entries of invertible factors. The finite table and prime can be compiled into the machine. The factorizations specialize modulo \\(q^b\\), with triangular factors invertible.</p><p>A lower triangular transformation of \\(H\\) or \\(D\\) costs \\(O(V)\\): update coordinates in descending physical order, multiply by the diagonal unit, and add a fixed linear combination of still unchanged earlier coordinates. Proposition 1 applies.</p><p>For \\(A=E_1\\Pi E_2\\), transform \\(D\\) by \\(E_2\\), \\(H\\) by \\(E_1^{-1}\\), add \\(\\Pi D\\) to \\(H\\), then restore using \\(E_1,E_2^{-1}\\). For each one in position \\((i,j)\\), implement \\(H_i\\leftarrow H_i+D_j\\) by\n\\[\nD_j\\leftarrow D_j+H_i,\\qquad\n(H_i,D_j)\\leftarrow(D_j,H_i),\\qquad\nD_j\\leftarrow H_i-D_j.\n\\]\nThe first and last updates target a later field. Starting with \\((h,d)\\), the sequence gives \\((h+d,d)\\), using one interchange. There are \\(a\\) ones.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Proposition 11, “Power-width interchange with rows.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The chunks can be interchanged, preserving all other address fields, in \\(O(Ve^\\tau)\\) time on fixed tapes.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A fixed pointwise bit circuit on \\(W\\) roles implements a role permutation \\(\\varrho\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fixed rational matrices are assigned to sources, gates, and sinks, common to all incidences of a gate.</p><p class=\"li\">For edge \\(e\\), let\n  \\[\n  A_e=M_{\\operatorname{head}(e)}-M_{\\operatorname{tail}(e)},\\qquad\n  s=\\sum_e\\operatorname{rank}_{\\mathbb Q}A_e.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Require\n  \\[\n  M_{\\operatorname{out}(\\varrho(w))}-M_{\\operatorname{in}(w)}=I_m,\n  \\qquad s&lt;Wm.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(q\\) as in Lemma 3.2, also avoiding denominators of all assigned matrices.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose rational \\(0&lt;\\tau&lt;1\\) with \\(s/W&lt;m^\\tau\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The two target chunks have \\(e=m^k\\) radix-\\(q\\) digits, \\(k\\ge0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A row field preceding both chunks has range length divisible by \\(W^k\\). Other fields may occur before or after the row field, between chunks, and after them.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The chunks can be interchanged, preserving all other address fields, in \\(O(Ve^\\tau)\\) time on fixed tapes.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(k=0\\), use Proposition 1’s single-digit operation. Otherwise split each chunk into \\(m\\) fields of \\(b=e/m\\) digits, giving vectors \\(H,D\\).</p><p>To implement \\(H\\leftarrow H+D\\), distribute rows cyclically into \\(W\\) role streams. A row \\(r=Wg+(w-1)\\) goes to role \\(w\\), with local row \\(g\\). Every role has identical remaining shape, volume \\(V/W\\), and row count divisible by \\(W^{k-1}\\).</p><p>For a rational matrix \\(M\\), let \\(\\Phi_M\\) move an entry from \\((H,D)\\) to \\((H+MD,D)\\). Then\n\\[\n\\Phi_{M'}\\Phi_M=\\Phi_{M'+M}.\n\\]\nOn edge \\(e\\), apply \\(\\Phi_{A_e}\\) using Lemma 3.2 and recursive pivot-pair interchanges. Apply circuit gates pointwise on aligned role addresses.</p><p>The frame identity proved in Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\nf'_{\\varrho(w)}\n=\\Phi_{M_{\\rm out}(\\varrho(w))}\n\\Phi_{-M_{\\rm in}(w)}f_w\n=\\Phi_{I_m}f_w.\n\\]\nMerge output role \\(\\varrho(w)\\) back into the original row class \\(w\\). This gives the same shear on every row, including auxiliary-role rows with arbitrary initial data.</p><p>Complete the interchange by\n\\[\nD\\leftarrow D-H,\\qquad H\\leftarrow H+D,\\qquad D\\leftarrow H-D.\n\\]\nOnly the middle shear uses the circuit; the others target later fields and are linear-cost.</p><p>There are exactly \\(s\\) child calls, each on volume \\(V/W\\). The fixed number of splits, merges, gates, and triangular operations costs \\(O(V)\\). If \\(F_k\\) is time divided by logical volume, then\n\\[\nF_0=O(1),\\qquad F_k\\le(s/W)F_{k-1}+O(1).\n\\]\nThe geometric sum is \\(O(m^{k\\tau})\\), including \\(s/W\\le1\\), so \\(F_k=O(e^\\tau)\\).</p><p>For a fixed-tape depth-first schedule, reserve \\(W\\) role tapes, fixed work tapes, a parking stack, and a descriptor stack. Before entering a child, park the \\(W-1\\) inactive streams, copy the active stream into the child area, clear parent role areas, and push the parent descriptor and finite program counter. On return, restore the active role and pop inactive streams in reverse order, writing popped symbols right-to-left to restore their original orientation. Clear only the current call’s visited regions. No scan traverses a parked ancestor.</p><p>Each parking, copying, positioning, erasing, or restoring operation costs \\(O(V)\\) at the parent. The number \\(s\\) of children is fixed.</p><p>Descriptor work also fits when the payload is one bit. If the root width is \\(e_0=m^{k_0}\\), root volume is \\(V_0\\), and depth-\\(j\\) volume is \\(V_j=V_0/W^j\\), then both original chunk ranges remain present as active fields or spectators:\n\\[\nV_j\\ge q^{2e_0},\\qquad\nj\\log W\\le(\\log_m e_0)\\log W=O(e_0)=O(\\log V_j).\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\log V_0=O(\\log(2V_j)).\n\\]\nA child descriptor retains its target fields and collapses consecutive spectators, so its fixed number of integer calculations costs\n\\[\n(\\log(2V_0))^{O(1)}=O(V_j).\n\\]\nThus the implementation uses one finite machine for all depths.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Lemma 13, “Packed selected-bit addition on one rectangle.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One fixed multitape procedure performs \\( y_{\\rho+iK}\\leftarrow y_{\\rho+iK}\\oplus x_{\\rho+iK} \\qquad(0\\le i<f), \\) preserving spectators, all other chunk bits, and every payload, and restoring \\(z\\) for every initial …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(R\\ge1\\), \\(K\\ge6\\), \\(f\\ge1\\), \\(L=fK\\), \\(0\\le\\rho&lt;K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N_0,N_1,N_2,N_3\\) be positive integers, and let \\((\\chi_1,\\chi_2,\\chi_3)\\) be a permutation of \\((x,y,z)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Records have \\(R\\) bits and are stored lexicographically on\n  \\[\n  [N_0]\\times[2^L]_{\\chi_1}\\times[N_1]\\times[2^L]_{\\chi_2}\n  \\times[N_2]\\times[2^L]_{\\chi_3}\\times[N_3].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The input supplies these lengths, \\(R,K,f,\\rho\\), and the finite permutation of names.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  M=2^{3L}\\prod_{h=0}^3N_h,\\qquad\n  V=MR,\\qquad A_{\\rm rect}=\\lceil\\log_2(2V)\\rceil,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\delta_{\\rm bad}=\\min\\{1,80(f-1)2^{-K}\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One fixed multitape procedure performs\n\\[\ny_{\\rho+iK}\\leftarrow y_{\\rho+iK}\\oplus x_{\\rho+iK}\n\\qquad(0\\le i&lt;f),\n\\]\npreserving spectators, all other chunk bits, and every payload, and restoring \\(z\\) for every initial value.</p><p>Its time is\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(\nV(L^\\tau+1)+MA_{\\rm rect}^3+\n\\delta_{\\rm bad}MA_{\\rm rect}(R+A_{\\rm rect})\n\\right).\n\\]\nFor a fixed parameter family with \\(A_{\\rm rect}=O(p)\\), \\(R\\) exceeding every fixed polynomial in \\(p\\), \\(f\\le d\\le p\\), and \\(K/\\log p\\to\\infty\\), the time is \\(O(V(L^\\tau+1))\\), uniformly over its permitted lengths and payloads.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a Boolean control \\(c\\) and arbitrary integers \\(u,w\\), perform, in chronological order,\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nu\\leftarrow u-c[w\\equiv0\\pmod2],&amp;\nw\\leftarrow w-c[u\\equiv0\\pmod2],\\\\\nu\\leftarrow u+c[w\\equiv0\\pmod2],&amp;\nw\\leftarrow w+c[u\\equiv0\\pmod2],\\\\\nw\\leftarrow w+c[u\\equiv1\\pmod2],&amp;\nu\\leftarrow u+c[w\\equiv1\\pmod2],\\\\\nw\\leftarrow w-c[u\\equiv1\\pmod2],&amp;\nu\\leftarrow u-c[w\\equiv1\\pmod2].\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nEvery predicate uses current values. For \\(c=0\\), nothing happens. For \\(c=1\\), the first four operations induce \\((00\\ 01\\ 10)\\), and the last four induce \\((01\\ 10\\ 11)\\), in each square\n\\[\n\\{2a,2a+1\\}\\times\\{2b,2b+1\\}.\n\\]\nTheir composition is \\((00\\ 10)(01\\ 11)\\). Thus\n\\[\n(u',w')=\\bigl(u+c(1-2(u\\bmod2)),w\\bigr).\n\\]\nBoth quotients under division by two are preserved, and \\(w\\) is restored. Each coordinate is updated four times by at most one; the weaker displacement bound eight is used below.</p><p>Temporarily omit the highest selected position \\(j_{f-1}\\), where \\(j_i=\\rho+iK\\). For \\(0\\le i&lt;f-1\\), let the \\(K\\)-bit segments beginning at \\(j_i\\) in \\(y,z\\) have initial values\n\\[\nu_i=2g_i+a_i,\\qquad w_i=2h_i+b_i.\n\\]\nApply each of the eight lines simultaneously. For example, a line updating \\(y\\) with sign \\(\\epsilon\\) and testing parity \\(b\\) of \\(z\\) is\n\\[\ny\\leftarrow y+\\epsilon\n\\sum_{i=0}^{f-2}2^{j_i}[x_{j_i}=1][z_{j_i}=b]\\pmod{2^L}.\n\\]\nIts offset is independent of its target, so it is a bijection. Let \\(S_0\\) be the eight-line composition.</p><p>Let \\(B\\) be the addresses where at least one used guard \\(g_i\\) or \\(h_i\\) fails\n\\[\n10\\le g\\le2^{K-1}-11.\n\\]\nOutside \\(B\\), every segment begins in \\([20,2^K-21]\\). Displacement eight cannot leave its segment range, so packed additions agree with independent segment additions without intersegment carry or borrow. Hence \\(S_0\\) agrees there with the desired lower-position XOR permutation \\(T_0\\).</p><p>Each guard excludes twenty values. The union bound over \\(2(f-1)\\) guards gives\n\\[\n|B|\\le\\delta_{\\rm bad}M.\n\\]\nThis count depends only on addresses. The ideal \\(T_0\\) preserves all guards and therefore \\(B\\). Since \\(S_0,T_0\\) are bijections agreeing off \\(B\\),\n\\[\nS_0(B^c)=T_0(B^c)=B^c,\\qquad S_0(B)=B.\n\\]\nConsequently \\(T_0S_0^{-1}\\) fixes \\(B^c\\) and permutes \\(B\\).</p><p>To execute a rotation, move its target to the rightmost of the three chunk positions using Proposition 3, at cost \\(O(VL^\\tau)\\), then apply Proposition 1’s controlled shift and undo the interchange. Collapse spectators into a fixed number of canonical lengths. Computing the offset takes \\(L^{O(1)}\\) time and is absorbed by the \\(2^L\\) target blocks. There are at most sixteen interchanges and eight rotations.</p><p>To repair \\(B\\), scan with a record-rank counter. Divide the rank through the fixed seven field lengths to recover chunk values and record strides. All integers have \\(O(A_{\\rm rect})\\) bits; schoolbook arithmetic, membership tests, copying, and resets cost \\(O(A_{\\rm rect}^3)\\) per record.</p><p>Extract precisely the records currently at exceptional addresses \\(q\\), mark their holes, and compute\n\\[\nq'=T_0(S_0^{-1}(q))\n\\]\nby reversing the eight modular additions with negated offsets and then applying the selected XORs. If \\(\\sigma_h\\) is chunk \\(h\\)’s record stride, then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(q')\n=\\operatorname{rank}(q)+\n\\sum_{h\\in\\{x,y,z\\}}(q'_h-q_h)\\sigma_h.\n\\]\nAttach this destination rank as a key. Stable binary radix sorting makes at most \\(A_{\\rm rect}\\) passes over only the extracted bank. The keys are distinct and exactly the marked-hole ranks. A forward full-stream scan reinserts the records.</p><p>Finally apply the XOR at \\(j_{f-1}\\) using Proposition 1. For \\(f=1\\), this is the whole procedure.</p><p>Movement costs \\(O(V(L^\\tau+1))\\); full record arithmetic costs \\(O(MA_{\\rm rect}^3+V)\\); exceptional sorting costs\n\\[\nO(|B|A_{\\rm rect}(R+A_{\\rm rect})).\n\\]\nAll counters, rewinds, and cleanup are included. Dividing the extra terms by \\(V=MR\\) bounds them by\n\\[\nA_{\\rm rect}^3/R,\\qquad\n80(f-1)2^{-K}A_{\\rm rect}(1+A_{\\rm rect}/R).\n\\]\nUnder the stated family assumptions these tend to zero.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 14, “Simultaneous normalized butterfly layer.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For all \\(p\\) beyond one fixed cutoff, one fixed multitape procedure returns \\(Q_p(\\mathsf Hz)\\) in the same format in \\( O(Vd^{\\lambda'}) \\) time, uniformly over all permitted parameters and values.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix\n  \\[\n  \\tau=\\sigma=1-2^{-50},\\qquad\\beta=\\tfrac12,\\qquad C_1=20.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix positive rational \\(c,\\epsilon,\\lambda,\\lambda'\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\max\\{\\tau,\\sigma\\}&lt;\\lambda&lt;1,\\qquad\n  \\tau(1+c/\\beta)&lt;\\lambda,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\max\\{\\lambda,\\sigma+\\beta(1-\\sigma)\\}&lt;\\lambda'&lt;1,\\qquad\n  \\epsilon C_1&lt;1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix positive constants \\(a_d\\le b_d\\), \\(a_r\\le b_r\\), \\(C_M,C_{\\rm desc}\\), and \\(C_w&gt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p,d,r\\) be positive integers with\n  \\[\n  a_dp^\\epsilon\\le d\\le b_dp^\\epsilon,\\qquad\n  2^{a_rp/d}\\le r\\le2^{b_rp/d},\n  \\qquad K=\\lfloor d^c\\rfloor.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(1\\le D\\le d\\), \\(P,S\\ge1\\), \\(0\\le\\rho&lt;K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The input header is\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal E=\n  \\Gamma(p)\\Gamma(d)\\Gamma(r)\\Gamma(D)\\Gamma(P)\\Gamma(S)\n  \\Gamma(K)\\Gamma(\\rho+1)\\Gamma(w).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The complete array address set is\n  \\[\n  [P]\\times[2^K]^D\\times[S],\\qquad M=PS2^{KD}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Each record is \\(z(x)=\\sum_{j=0}^{r-1}z_{x,j}y^j\\), in coefficient order, with \\(|z_{x,j}|\\le1\\) and real and imaginary parts in \\(2^{-p}\\mathbb Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Each component is \\(2^{-p}\\) times a signed \\(w\\)-bit two’s-complement integer, most significant bit first; real precedes imaginary.</p><p class=\"li\">Require\n  \\[\n  \\log_2(2M)\\le C_Mp,\\qquad\n  |\\mathcal E|\\le C_{\\rm desc}p,\\qquad\n  p+2\\le w\\le C_wp.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathsf H\\) be the tensor product of\n  \\[\n  H_0=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix}\n  \\]\n  on bit \\(\\rho\\), numbered from the least significant end, in each of the \\(D\\) chunks. Let \\(Q_p\\) truncate each real and imaginary coefficient part toward zero to \\(2^{-p}\\mathbb Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(V=2Mrw\\) and\n  \\[\n  C_0=128m\\bigl(s_c+64(W_c+m+1)^3\\bigr)^2.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For all \\(p\\) beyond one fixed cutoff, one fixed multitape procedure returns \\(Q_p(\\mathsf Hz)\\) in the same format in\n\\[\nO(Vd^{\\lambda'})\n\\]\ntime, uniformly over all permitted parameters and values.</p><p>Every returned coefficient is in the unit disk and\n\\[\n\\|Q_p(\\mathsf Hz)-\\mathsf Hz\\|_\\infty&lt;\\sqrt2\\,2^{-p}.\n\\]\nThe entire layer is evaluated exactly before its final truncation.</p><p>Internally, at most\n\\[\np+\\lceil C_0d^{C_1}\\rceil\n\\]\nfractional bits and\n\\[\n\\lceil C_0d^{C_1}\\rceil+3\n\\]\ninteger and sign bits per real component suffice. These widths are \\(O(p)\\). Time includes addressing, deterministic repair, row padding and removal, and cleanup.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 5.1–5.3 supply the exact operation, volume-normalized recurrence, fixed-tape scheduling, and sufficient width.</p><p>For uniformity, put \\(\\Delta=\\lceil C_0d^{C_1}\\rceil\\). Beyond one common cutoff, \\(\\Delta\\le p\\), and a guarded polynomial record has\n\\[\nR_g=2r(p+2\\Delta+3),\\qquad 2rp\\le R_g\\le12rp.\n\\]\nPadding at most doubles the record count, and splitting only decreases it. Hence every packed child has\n\\[\nA_*=\\lceil\\log_2(2M_{\\rm child}R_g)\\rceil\n\\le C_Ap,\\qquad C_A=C_M+b_r+7.\n\\]\nThe comparison bands give, uniformly,\n\\[\nf\\le d\\le p,\\qquad\nK\\ge\\tfrac12a_d^cp^{\\epsilon c},\\qquad\nr\\ge2^{(a_r/b_d)p^{1-\\epsilon}}.\n\\]\nThus \\(K\\ge6\\), \\(K/\\log p\\to\\infty\\), and \\(r\\) dominates every fixed polynomial. Proposition 4’s additional normalized terms satisfy\n\\[\nA_*^3/R_g\\le C_A^3p^2/(2r),\n\\]\n\\[\n80(f-1)2^{-K}A_*(1+A_*/R_g)\n\\le80C_Ap^2\\,2^{-K}(1+C_A/(2r)).\n\\]\nBoth tend uniformly to zero, establishing the claimed packed-call constants.</p><p>Convert the supplied words to the guarded format by changing only redundant sign bits and appending fractional zeros. Lemma 5.1 converts the exact \\(C\\)-layer to \\(\\mathsf H\\). At the end, truncate by shifting absolute numerators and restoring signs.</p><p>Every row of \\(\\mathsf H\\) has absolute row sum one, so it maps disk arrays to disk arrays. Componentwise truncation cannot increase either component’s absolute value and changes each by less than \\(2^{-p}\\). Therefore the output remains in the disk and has error less than \\(\\sqrt2\\,2^{-p}\\). Its numerator magnitudes are at most \\(2^p\\), fitting the supplied \\(w\\)-bit format.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Sections 5.1 and 3.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "One invocation of the finite phase network reduces \\(C^{\\otimes e}\\) to exactly \\(s_c\\) child operations on \\(f=e/m\\) selected bits, plus a fixed number of binary row additions on corresponding slot bits …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 5’s selected-bit setting and Proposition 2’s complex network.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  S=\\operatorname{diag}(1,i),\\quad Z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1),\\quad\n  C=aI+bX,\\quad a=(1+i)/2,\\quad b=(1-i)/2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Consider a group of \\(e=mf\\) consecutive axis chunks, divided into \\(m\\) slots of \\(f\\) chunks. Corresponding selected bits in the slots form \\(f\\) columns of \\(\\mathbb F_2^m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One invocation of the finite phase network reduces \\(C^{\\otimes e}\\) to exactly \\(s_c\\) child operations on \\(f=e/m\\) selected bits, plus a fixed number of binary row additions on corresponding slot bits, coefficientwise scalar operations, and endpoint corrections.</p><p>Moreover, if\n\\[\n(S_Dz)(x)=i^{x_1+\\cdots+x_D}z(x)\n\\]\nfor the selected bits \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_D\\), then\n\\[\nH_0^{\\otimes D}=b^D S_D C^{\\otimes D}S_D.\n\\]\nAll wrappers and corrections use Gaussian dyadic operations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Direct multiplication gives\n\\[\nH_0=bSCS,\\qquad C^2=X,\\qquad C^4=I,\\qquad\nC^{-1}=-iZCZ.\n\\]</p><p>For a residual basis \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_a\\), Proposition 2 gives\n\\[\nC_{U_+}C_{U_-}^{-1}=\\prod_{j=1}^a C_{v_j}^{\\epsilon_j},\n\\qquad C_v=aI+bX_v,\\quad\\epsilon_j\\in\\{1,-1\\}.\n\\]\nExtend the basis to the columns of \\(M\\in\\mathrm{GL}(m,\\mathbb F_2)\\). Let \\(P_M\\) move the value at \\(x\\) to \\(Mx\\). Then\n\\[\nP_MX_vP_M^{-1}=X_{Mv},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nC_{U_+}C_{U_-}^{-1}\n=P_M\\left(\\prod_{j=1}^aC_{e_j}^{\\epsilon_j}\\right)P_M^{-1}.\n\\]\nApply \\(P_M^{-1}\\) to every column, one child operation on each indicated slot, and \\(P_M\\) to every column. An inverse child uses one forward child because\n\\[\n(C^{-1})^{\\otimes f}=(-i)^fZ^{\\otimes f}C^{\\otimes f}Z^{\\otimes f}.\n\\]</p><p>Gaussian elimination expresses a fixed-order \\(M\\) using at most \\(m(m-1)+3m\\) row additions; a swap is three additions. Each row addition changes corresponding selected bits in two slots and is implemented by Proposition 4 using any third slot as auxiliary, which is restored.</p><p>Summing residual dimensions gives exactly \\(s_c\\) children. Proposition 2’s source and sink support signs, \\(i^{27f}\\) sink phases, bank signs, and inverse routing make every endpoint \\(C^{\\otimes e}\\), including on auxiliary roles.</p><p>Tensoring \\(H_0=bSCS\\) gives the final identity. Since\n\\[\nb^D=(-i/2)^{\\lfloor D/2\\rfloor}b^{D\\bmod2},\n\\]\nthe scale is a binary shift and fourth-root phase, with at most one further factor \\(b\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Section 5.3, “Batching a common layer on a fixed number of tapes.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The recursive implementation of \\(C^{\\otimes D}\\), with all values retained exactly, has total time \\(O(Vd^{\\lambda'})\\), provided the common format represents every intermediate value exactly.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 5’s parameter family and array layout.</p><p class=\"li\">Temporarily assume a common exact coefficient format of width \\(O(p)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(W=W_c\\), \\(s=s_c\\), and \\(m=10^6\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The recursive implementation of \\(C^{\\otimes D}\\), with all values retained exactly, has total time \\(O(Vd^{\\lambda'})\\), provided the common format represents every intermediate value exactly.</p><p>The recursion uses only a fixed number of tapes, and each child has exactly \\(1/W\\) of its parent’s logical volume.</p>", "proof": "<p>An individual selected-bit kernel splits the stream on that bit, aligns the two remaining address sets, and computes coefficientwise\n\\[\nC(u,v)=\n\\left(\n\\frac{u+v+i(u-v)}2,\\,\n\\frac{u+v-i(u-v)}2\n\\right).\n\\]\nThe paired coefficient scan, split, merge, reversals, and head returns cost \\(O(V)\\). Thus \\(e\\) individual kernels cost \\(O(Ve)\\).</p><p>At an internal group of \\(e=m^k\\) active chunks, Lemma 5.1 supplies exactly \\(s\\) calls on \\(e/m\\) chunks. Proposition 4 implements each binary basis change in\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(V((eK)^\\tau+1)\\right).\n\\]\nThe network, bases, row additions, scalar gates, and corrections are fixed, so this bounds total node overhead.</p><p>Put\n\\[\nq_0=\\lceil\\log_2W\\rceil\\lceil\\log_m(2d)\\rceil,\\qquad\nk_0=\\lceil\\log_m d\\rceil.\n\\]\nIf \\(D\\le q_0\\), use individual kernels, costing \\(O(V\\log d)\\). Otherwise process the first \\(q_0\\) selected bits individually, and regard their full chunks as one preceding row field. Its range\n\\[\nR_{\\rm row}=2^{q_0K}\\ge W^{k_0}\n\\]\ncan be padded to the next multiple of \\(W^{k_0}\\), increasing volume by at most two.</p><p>At a node, write a row index as \\(Wg+w\\) and send the whole row to role \\(w\\). Every role has identical complete suffix and volume \\(V/W\\); at depth \\(j\\), row counts remain divisible by \\(W^{k_0-j}\\). No address within a row is removed. Inactive chunks remain complete spectators, so Proposition 4 applies in every child.</p><p>Park inactive role streams on a LIFO tape before recursion and restore them afterward, using the fixed role tapes at every depth. The stack head remains at its active top; no step traverses ancestor data. A separate stack stores descriptors and finite program counters. Parking, copying, clearing, and restoring cost \\(O(V)\\) at each node.</p><p>Each child still contains a full length-\\(r\\) polynomial record. The complete address and collapsed descriptors have \\(O(p)\\) bits; polynomial descriptor work per record is absorbed by \\(\\Theta(rp)\\) payload bits. There are only \\(d^{O(1)}\\) nodes, since branching is fixed and depth is \\(O(\\log d)\\).</p><p>Stop when \\(e&lt;d^\\beta\\). For time divided by logical volume,\n\\[\nF(e)\\le(s/W)F(e/m)+O((eK)^\\tau+1),\\qquad\nF(e)=O(e)\\quad(e&lt;d^\\beta).\n\\]\nAt an internal node, \\(K\\le d^c\\le e^{c/\\beta}\\), so overhead is \\(O(e^\\lambda)\\). With \\(a=s/W\\le m^\\sigma&lt;m^\\lambda\\), internal costs sum to\n\\[\nO(e^\\lambda)\\sum_{j\\ge0}(a/m^\\lambda)^j=O(e^\\lambda).\n\\]\nAt leaves of size \\(u=e/m^j&lt;d^\\beta\\), the combined normalized contribution is\n\\[\nO(a^ju)\\le O(e^\\sigma u^{1-\\sigma})\n\\le O(e^\\sigma d^{\\beta(1-\\sigma)}).\n\\]</p><p>Partition \\(D-q_0\\) into base-\\(m\\) pieces. There are \\(O(\\log(2d))\\) pieces, with fixed digit multiplicity. Including preprocessing, the normalized cost is\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(\n\\log(2d)\\,[d^\\lambda+d^{\\sigma+\\beta(1-\\sigma)}+1]\n\\right)=O(d^{\\lambda'}).\n\\]</p><p>Completed invocations apply \\(C^{\\otimes e}\\) separately to every original row. Their selected bits are disjoint from the preprocessing row bits, so the completed operations commute. Padded zero rows are zero again at invocation boundaries and may then be removed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Section 5.4, “An explicit guard-width bound.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the stated network, \\(m^{10}<B<m^{11}\\), so \\(\\nu=11\\) and \\(C_1=20\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the exact recursive computation of Lemmas 5.1–5.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  E=64(W_c+m+1)^3,\\qquad B=s_c+E,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\nu=\\min\\{j\\ge1:m^j\\ge B\\},\\qquad\n  C_1=\\max\\{20,\\nu+3\\},\\qquad C_0=128mB^2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Arithmetic dependency depth counts complex additions, subtractions, halvings, sign changes, and multiplication by \\(i\\). A shift by \\(t\\) fractional places is charged \\(t\\) denominator-growth units.</p><p class=\"li\">Inputs lie in the unit disk on the \\(2^{-p}\\mathbb Z[i]\\) grid.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For the stated network, \\(m^{10}&lt;B&lt;m^{11}\\), so \\(\\nu=11\\) and \\(C_1=20\\).</p><p>Both denominator growth and logarithmic magnitude growth throughout one complete normalized layer are less than \\(C_0d^{C_1}\\). Thus the widths in Proposition 5 represent all intermediates exactly.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every data wire occurs in at most twelve scalar gates across the three stages, and every auxiliary wire in at most four. There are at most \\(12W_c\\) gates. A gate has at most \\(W_c\\) inputs and outputs and coefficients in \\(\\{0,\\pm1,\\pm1/2\\}\\); evaluating saved-input output combinations takes at most \\(2W_c^2\\) elementary operations.</p><p>Inverse-child corrections contribute at most \\(4s_c\\) operations; endpoint and bank corrections at most \\(4W_c+4\\). Since \\(s_c&lt;W_cm\\), their total is less than \\(E\\).</p><p>If \\(A(e)\\) bounds a coefficient’s dependency depth in an \\(e\\)-axis recursive layer, conservatively concatenating all calls gives\n\\[\nA(e)\\le s_cA(e/m)+E\n\\]\ninternally, and \\(A(e)\\le8e\\) at a leaf. With at most \\(k\\le\\lceil\\log_m d\\rceil\\) internal levels,\n\\[\nA(e)\\le8dB^{k+1}\\le8B^2d^{\\nu+1}.\n\\]\nConcatenating at most \\(md\\) base-\\(m\\) pieces gives \\(8mB^2d^{\\nu+2}\\); preprocessing adds at most \\(8d\\).</p><p>The two outer diagonals cost at most four phase operations. Writing \\(t=\\lfloor D/2\\rfloor\\), the final factor\n\\[\nb^D=(-i)^t2^{-t}b^{D\\bmod2}\n\\]\nadds a phase, at most one \\((z-iz)/2\\), and \\(t\\) units for the shift. The common bound is\n\\[\n8mB^2d^{\\nu+2}+8d+t+9\n\\le26mB^2d^{\\nu+2}\n&lt;C_0d^{C_1}.\n\\]</p><p>A value at depth \\(\\ell\\) lies in \\(2^{-(p+\\ell)}\\mathbb Z[i]\\) and has modulus at most \\(2^\\ell\\): addition or subtraction at most doubles the maximum operand modulus; halving adds at most one denominator bit; signs and multiplication by \\(i\\) preserve these bounds. Therefore reserving the stated fractional and integer bits prevents overflow and discards no information.</p><p>Address interchanges may temporarily XOR encoding bits, but coefficient arithmetic resumes only after the exact encoding permutation has completed. These bit operations cause no numerical growth or rounding.</p><p>Since \\(d=\\Theta(p^\\epsilon)\\) and \\(\\epsilon C_1&lt;1\\),\n\\[\nC_0d^{C_1}=o(p),\n\\]\nso the common exact width is \\(O(p)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Lemma 16, “Transform layout and cost.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Beyond one fixed cutoff, a fixed-tape procedure constructs an explicit positional record permutation \\(L\\) and approximates \\( LBF_{\\boldsymbol t}^-. \\) Its opposite procedure consumes that order and approximates …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix Proposition 5’s rational parameters and comparison constants.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(C_\\ell&gt;0,C_w&gt;1\\), and choose\n  \\[\n  C_M=C_\\ell+1,\\qquad C_{\\rm desc}=32(C_\\ell+C_w+1)\n  \\]\n  in Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p,d,r\\) satisfy its comparison bands, with\n  \\[\n  d\\ge2,\\qquad r=2^\\ell,\\qquad2\\le\\ell&lt;p,\\qquad\n  d\\ell\\le C_\\ell p,\\qquad1\\le K=\\lfloor d^c\\rfloor\\le\\ell-1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(t_i\\in\\{2^{\\ell-1},2^\\ell\\}\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le d-1\\), and put\n  \\[\n  M=\\prod_{i&lt;d}t_i,\\qquad T=rM.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(p+2\\le w\\le C_wp\\), and supply\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal T=\\Gamma(p)\\Gamma(d)\\Gamma(r)\\Gamma(K)\\Gamma(w)\n  \\prod_{i&lt;d}\\Gamma(t_i).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Records are disk grid polynomials in\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal R_r=\\mathbb C[y]/(y^r+1)\n  \\]\n  with degree less than \\(r\\), coefficient sup norm, and Proposition 5’s component format.</p><p class=\"li\">For a power of two \\(t\\mid2r\\), set \\(\\omega_t=y^{2r/t}\\), and\n  \\[\n  (F_t^-z)_j=\\frac1t\\sum_{k&lt;t}\\omega_t^{-jk}z_k,\\qquad\n  (F_t^+z)_j=\\frac1t\\sum_{k&lt;t}\\omega_t^{jk}z_k.\n  \\]\n  Tensor these maps over the \\(d-1\\) axes.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B_t\\) move index \\(j\\) to its bit reversal, and \\(B=\\bigotimes_{i&lt;d}B_{t_i}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Beyond one fixed cutoff, a fixed-tape procedure constructs an explicit positional record permutation \\(L\\) and approximates\n\\[\nLBF_{\\boldsymbol t}^-.\n\\]\nIts opposite procedure consumes that order and approximates\n\\[\nF_{\\boldsymbol t}^+B^{-1}L^{-1}.\n\\]\nEach returns disk grid coefficients in the original component format, has error less than\n\\[\n\\sqrt2\\,\\ell\\,2^{-p},\n\\]\nand costs\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(\nTp\\,[dK+pK^{\\tau-1}+\\ell d^{\\lambda'}]\n\\right).\n\\]\nLayout construction and final restoration are charged. The forward output layout may be retained through a pointwise product.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(D=d-1\\), \\(a_i=\\log_2t_i\\), and name slot \\((i,h)\\) in axis \\(i\\). Let\n\\[\nb=(\\ell-1)\\bmod K,\\qquad q=\\lfloor(\\ell-1)/K\\rfloor.\n\\]\nMove to a prefix:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. The slots \\((i,\\ell-1)\\) of long axes, in increasing \\(i\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The slots \\((i,h)\\), for \\(h=b-1,\\ldots,0\\), and within each \\(h\\) in increasing \\(i\\).</p><p>There are \\(O(dK)\\) such single-bit moves. Every remaining axis consists of \\(q\\) \\(K\\)-bit chunks. Exchange chunks to change axis-major order\n\\[\n(1,1),(1,2),\\ldots,(D,q)\n\\]\nto chunk-major order\n\\[\n(1,1),(2,1),\\ldots,(D,1),(1,2),\\ldots,(D,q).\n\\]\nSelecting the required chunk at each target position uses at most \\(Dq-1\\) interchanges. Proposition 3 charges\n\\[\nO(TpDqK^\\tau)=O(Tp\\,pK^{\\tau-1}).\n\\]\nThese moves define \\(L\\); recording inverse moves gives \\(L^{-1}\\) at the same cost.</p><p>Generate the named-slot and layout descriptors in polynomial time in \\(p\\). Since\n\\[\nT\\ge r\\ge2^{(a_r/b_d)p^{1-\\epsilon}},\n\\]\nthis setup is \\(O(Tp)\\).</p><p>Process the extra top round on long axes, then common positions \\(\\ell-2,\\ldots,b\\), then bottom prefix positions \\(b-1,\\ldots,0\\). In a chunk round, corresponding selected slots have common offset\n\\[\n(h-b)\\bmod K\n\\]\nin \\(D\\) consecutive chunks. The remaining bits collapse to a complete layout\n\\[\n[P]\\times[2^K]^D\\times[S].\n\\]\nIts record count is \\(M\\), and\n\\[\n\\log_2(2M)\\le(C_\\ell+1)p.\n\\]\nThe round header satisfies\n\\[\n|\\mathcal E|\n\\le2C_\\ell p+12\\log_2p\n+4\\log_2\\max\\{C_\\ell,1\\}+2\\log_2C_w+9\n\\le C_{\\rm desc}p.\n\\]\nThus Proposition 5 applies, at cost \\(O(Tpd^{\\lambda'})\\) per round.</p><p>On prefix slots, process each participating bit individually after temporarily moving it before the polynomial suffix. Pairwise half-sums and half-differences cost \\(O(Tp)\\) per bit. Evaluate the whole round exactly before truncation: at most \\(D\\) factors need \\(p+D+O(1)=O(p)\\) component bits. At most \\(K\\) prefix rounds cost \\(O(TpdK)\\).</p><p>For every record, calculate \\(E\\) from the named-slot descriptor in polynomial time in \\(p\\), absorbed by its \\(\\Theta(rp)\\) bits. To multiply by \\(y^E\\), write \\(E=er+s\\), \\(0\\le s&lt;r\\), split the coefficient list at \\(r-s\\), put the tail before the head, negate the moved tail, and apply \\((-1)^e\\). This costs \\(O(rp)\\) per record.</p><p>Because signed coefficient permutations commute with componentwise truncation,\n\\[\nJ_EQ_p(\\mathsf Hz)=Q_p(J_E\\mathsf Hz).\n\\]\nEach round is therefore a contraction followed by one truncation of error less than \\(\\sqrt2\\,2^{-p}\\). At most \\(\\ell\\) rounds give the stated error and preserve the disk.</p><p>Finally, write the exact logical procedures before relocation as\n\\[\nD^-=BF_{\\boldsymbol t}^-,\n\\qquad D^+=F_{\\boldsymbol t}^+B^{-1}.\n\\]\nExecuting named-slot operations after \\(L\\) conjugates them by \\(L\\). The full exact operators are\n\\[\n(LD^-L^{-1})L=LBF_{\\boldsymbol t}^-,\n\\]\n\\[\nL^{-1}(LD^+L^{-1})=F_{\\boldsymbol t}^+B^{-1}L^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe same record permutation on both forward outputs commutes with their pointwise product.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 15, “Synthetic character cancellation.”", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every integer \\(j\\), \\( \\sum_{k=0}^{t-1}\\omega_t^{jk} = \\begin{cases} t,&t\\mid j,\\\\ 0,&t\\nmid j. \\end{cases} \\) Consequently \\( F_t^+F_t^-=t^{-1}I. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in \\(\\mathcal R_r=\\mathbb C[y]/(y^r+1)\\), with \\(r\\) a power of two.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(t\\mid2r\\) be a power of two and \\(\\omega_t=y^{2r/t}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every integer \\(j\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=0}^{t-1}\\omega_t^{jk}\n=\n\\begin{cases}\nt,&amp;t\\mid j,\\\\\n0,&amp;t\\nmid j.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\nF_t^+F_t^-=t^{-1}I.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Only \\(t\\nmid j\\) needs proof. Write \\(j=2^vj_0\\), with \\(j_0\\) odd and \\(0\\le v&lt;\\log_2t\\). Replacing \\(k\\) by \\(k+t/2^{v+1}\\) permutes the summands and multiplies their sum by\n\\[\ny^{rj_0}=-1.\n\\]\nThe sum is zero because the additive group of \\(\\mathcal R_r\\) has no two-torsion. Expanding the composite transforms gives the final identity.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Section 6.1, including equations (27)–(29)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The normalized decimation-in-frequency procedure computes \\(B_tF_t^-\\) directly in bit-reversed frequency order.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 6’s synthetic roots and transforms.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t=2^a\\), name the original bit slots \\(a-1,\\ldots,0\\), from most to least significant.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The normalized decimation-in-frequency procedure computes \\(B_tF_t^-\\) directly in bit-reversed frequency order. Its reverse arithmetic procedure computes \\(F_t^+B_t^{-1}\\).</p><p>Across several axes, a common round is a simultaneous normalized butterfly \\(\\mathsf H\\), followed by multiplication of each record by \\(y^E\\), where\n\\[\nE=-\\sum_{i\\text{ participating}}\\frac{2r}{u_i}b_ik_i\\pmod{2r}.\n\\]\nHere \\(u_i\\) is the current recursive length, \\(b_i\\) the new branch bit, and \\(0\\le k_i&lt;u_i/2\\) is encoded by the still unprocessed lower named slots. An opposite round applies \\(y^{-E}\\) first and \\(\\mathsf H\\) second. Every completed round is a contraction.</p>", "proof": "<p>Pair \\(z_k,z_{k+t/2}\\), apply \\(H_0\\), multiply the second output by \\(\\omega_t^{-k}\\), and recurse in the two branches. For \\(b\\in\\{0,1\\}\\),\n\\[\n(F_t^-z)_{2h+b}\n=\\frac1{t/2}\\sum_{k&lt;t/2}\n(\\omega_t^2)^{-hk}\\omega_t^{-bk}\n\\frac{z_k+(-1)^bz_{k+t/2}}2.\n\\]\nAfter \\(s\\) levels, the first \\(s\\) named slots hold branch bits \\(b_0,\\ldots,b_{s-1}\\), which are frequency bits \\(0,\\ldots,s-1\\). The next slot is \\(h=a-1-s\\); its twiddle index is\n\\[\nk=\\sum_{v&lt;h}2^vx_v.\n\\]\nPreviously written branch bits do not enter \\(k\\). At completion, named slots from high to low contain frequency bits from low to high, establishing bit reversal without a separate frequency-index move.</p><p>Reverse the recursion: process smaller branches, undo the monomial, then apply \\(H_0\\). Since \\(H_0^2=I/2\\), each reversed level is half the inverse of its forward level. Over \\(a\\) levels the composite is \\(I/t\\). Lemma 6.1 identifies this reverse map as \\(F_t^+B_t^{-1}\\).</p><p>A twiddle for one axis depends only on that axis’s bits, so it commutes with the butterflies of other axes. Combining participating twiddles gives the displayed \\(E\\). Monomial multiplication is a signed coefficient permutation and hence an isometry; normalized butterflies are contractions.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 17, “Signed packing and normalized ring multiplication.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "One can compute the exact Gaussian integer numerators of \\(fg\\), then return \\( Q_p(fg/r) \\) in the same format in \\(O(rp\\log(rp))\\) time.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r=2^\\ell&lt;2^p\\), with \\(\\ell\\ge1,p\\ge2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(C_w&gt;1\\) and \\(p+2\\le w\\le C_wp\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Supply \\(\\Gamma(p)\\Gamma(r)\\Gamma(w)\\) and disk grid polynomials \\(f,g\\in\\mathcal R_r\\) in Proposition 5’s format.</p><p class=\"li\">The established Harvey–van der Hoeven multiplier computes exact \\(N\\)-bit integer products in \\(O(N\\log(2N))\\) time on fixed tapes and is used as an independent subroutine.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can compute the exact Gaussian integer numerators of \\(fg\\), then return\n\\[\nQ_p(fg/r)\n\\]\nin the same format in \\(O(rp\\log(rp))\\) time. The output is a disk grid polynomial and differs from \\(fg/r\\) by less than \\(\\sqrt2\\,2^{-p}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\nf=2^{-p}(a+ib),\\qquad g=2^{-p}(c+ie),\n\\]\nwith integer coefficient magnitudes at most \\(2^p\\). Put \\(B_0=2^{4p}\\) and\n\\[\nA_u=u(B_0)=\\sum_{j&lt;r}u_jB_0^j\n\\qquad(u\\in\\{a,b,c,e\\}).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n|A_u|\\le2^p\\frac{B_0^r-1}{B_0-1}&lt;B_0^r/2,\n\\]\nso magnitudes fit in \\(4pr\\) bits. Compute the four signed products\n\\[\nA_aA_c,\\quad A_bA_e,\\quad A_aA_e,\\quad A_bA_c.\n\\]\nFor an ordinary product \\(h=ac\\), for example,\n\\[\nA_aA_c=\\sum_{j=0}^{2r-2}h_jB_0^j,\\qquad\n|h_j|\\le r2^{2p}&lt;2^{3p}&lt;B_0/2.\n\\]</p><p>To pack \\(u\\), start \\(\\gamma_0=0\\) and write\n\\[\nd_j\\equiv u_j+\\gamma_j\\pmod{B_0},\\quad0\\le d_j&lt;B_0,\n\\qquad\n\\gamma_{j+1}=(u_j+\\gamma_j-d_j)/B_0.\n\\]\nThe carry stays in \\(\\{-1,0\\}\\), and\n\\[\nA_u=\\sum_{j&lt;r}d_jB_0^j+\\gamma_rB_0^r.\n\\]\nThese blocks form fixed-width two’s complement. Complement and increment when negative to obtain the magnitude. Packing and endianness changes are linear scans.</p><p>For extraction, put \\(J=2r\\), pad \\(h_{J-1}=0\\), and represent the signed product as\n\\[\nZ=\\sum_{j&lt;J}q_jB_0^j+\\varepsilon B_0^J,\n\\qquad0\\le q_j&lt;B_0,\\quad\\varepsilon\\in\\{-1,0\\}.\n\\]\nStart \\(\\rho_0=0\\). Let \\(\\widehat h_j\\) be the centered residue of \\(q_j+\\rho_j\\) in \\([-B_0/2,B_0/2)\\), and put\n\\[\n\\rho_{j+1}=(q_j+\\rho_j-\\widehat h_j)/B_0.\n\\]\nThe carry stays in \\(\\{0,1\\}\\). After lower coefficients have been extracted, the residual identity is\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=j}^{J-1}h_kB_0^{k-j}\n=\n\\sum_{k=j}^{J-1}q_kB_0^{k-j}+\\rho_j+\\varepsilon B_0^{J-j}.\n\\]\nReduction modulo \\(B_0\\), with the strict coefficient bound, identifies \\(\\widehat h_j=h_j\\). Subtraction and division by \\(B_0\\) prove the next identity. At \\(j=J\\), \\(0=\\rho_J+\\varepsilon\\). Thus extraction requires one block scan.</p><p>Form real and imaginary numerator streams \\(ac-be\\), \\(ae+bc\\). Reduce modulo \\(y^r+1\\) by subtracting coefficient \\(j+r\\) from coefficient \\(j\\). Each resulting Gaussian coefficient \\(H_j\\) is a sum of exactly \\(r\\) signed scalar products, so\n\\[\n|H_j|\\le r2^{2p},\\qquad\n\\|fg/r\\|_\\infty\\le\\|f\\|_\\infty\\|g\\|_\\infty.\n\\]\nAll coefficient arithmetic uses \\(O(p)\\) bits.</p><p>The returned grid numerator is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{trunc}_0\\!\\left(\\frac{\\Re H_j}{2^{p+\\ell}}\\right)\n+i\\operatorname{trunc}_0\\!\\left(\\frac{\\Im H_j}{2^{p+\\ell}}\\right).\n\\]\nShift absolute magnitudes and restore signs. This is exactly \\(Q_p(2^{-2p}H_j/r)\\), preserves the disk, and has the stated truncation error. Four integer products dominate all linear scans.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Proposition 18, “Convolution from the retained transform layout.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For disk grid operands, a fixed-tape procedure approximates \\(\\mathcal M_r\\) with error less than \\( \\sqrt2\\,M(3\\ell+1)2^{-p} \\) and cost \\( O\\!\\left( Tp[dK+pK^{\\tau-1}+\\ell d^{\\lambda'}+\\log(rp)] \\right). \\) It returns …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use all hypotheses and component formats of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">For arrays on \\(\\prod_{i&lt;d}\\mathbb Z/t_i\\mathbb Z\\) with values in \\(\\mathcal R_r\\), let \\(*\\) denote cyclic convolution in those axes.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal M_r(f,g)=\\frac{f*g}{rM}=\\frac{f*g}{T},\n  \\qquad m_M=\\log_2M,\\qquad w_M=w+m_M.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For disk grid operands, a fixed-tape procedure approximates \\(\\mathcal M_r\\) with error less than\n\\[\n\\sqrt2\\,M(3\\ell+1)2^{-p}\n\\]\nand cost\n\\[\nO\\!\\left(\nTp[dK+pK^{\\tau-1}+\\ell d^{\\lambda'}+\\log(rp)]\n\\right).\n\\]\nIt returns the original array and coefficient order, with signed component width \\(w_M\\), and supplies \\(\\Gamma(w_M)\\).</p><p>If one operand has norm at most \\(\\rho&lt;1\\) and the error bound is at most \\(1-\\rho\\), the returned array is a disk array.</p>", "proof": "<p>A fixed output coefficient of \\(\\mathcal M_r\\) averages \\(rM\\) signed products, giving\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal M_r(f,g)\\|_\\infty\\le\\|f\\|_\\infty\\|g\\|_\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>Apply both forward transforms of Proposition 6 and retain their common \\(LB\\) order. Apply Proposition 7 to corresponding polynomial records, returning their product divided by \\(r\\) and truncated. Apply the opposite transform to that order, then multiply exactly by \\(M\\).</p><p>The normalization is\n\\[\nF_{\\boldsymbol t}^-(f*g)\n=M(F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-f)(F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-g),\n\\qquad\nF_{\\boldsymbol t}^+F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-=I/M,\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\nM F_{\\boldsymbol t}^+\n\\left(\\frac{(F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-f)(F_{\\boldsymbol t}^-g)}r\\right)\n=\\frac{f*g}{rM}.\n\\]\nThe common record permutation commutes with pointwise multiplication.</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\delta=\\sqrt2\\,\\ell\\,2^{-p},\\qquad\\eta=\\sqrt2\\,2^{-p}.\n\\]\nExact and computed forward outputs are disk arrays. The normalized polynomial product is a bilinear contraction, so the two forward errors contribute less than \\(2\\delta\\); product truncation adds less than \\(\\eta\\). The opposite contraction and its computation add less than \\(\\delta\\). Exact scaling by \\(M\\) gives error less than\n\\[\nM(3\\delta+\\eta).\n\\]\nThe smaller-operand condition gives output norm less than \\(\\rho+(1-\\rho)=1\\).</p><p>Compute \\(m_M=\\sum_{i&lt;d}\\log_2t_i\\) and append \\(m_M\\) least significant zeros to each signed numerator, producing exact width \\(w_M\\), including negative numerators. This preserves the grid and costs \\(O(Tp)\\).</p><p>There are \\(M=T/r\\) polynomial products, totaling \\(O(Tp\\log(rp))\\). The three transform costs and all scaling scans give the stated bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma 19, “Resampling without executing the two permutations.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are linear contractions \\(A:\\mathbb C^s\\to\\mathbb C^t\\) and \\(B_0:\\mathbb C^t\\to\\mathbb C^s\\) such that \\( P_sF_s=2^{2\\alpha^2}B_0P_tF_tA. \\) Fixed multitape algorithms …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(0&lt;\\delta&lt;1/8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let integers \\(p&gt;100\\), \\(2\\le s&lt;t&lt;2^p\\), and \\(2\\le\\alpha&lt;\\sqrt p\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\gcd(s,t)=1,\\qquad\\theta=t/s-1&gt;p/\\alpha^4.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define normalized complex Fourier transforms\n  \\[\n  (F_qu)_k=\\frac1q\\sum_{j&lt;q}u_je^{-2\\pi ijk/q},\n  \\qquad\n  (F_q^+u)_k=\\frac1q\\sum_{j&lt;q}u_je^{2\\pi ijk/q}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb D_p=\\{2^{-p}(a+ib):a,b\\in\\mathbb Z,\\ a^2+b^2\\le2^{2p}\\}.\n  \\]\n  Use signed \\(w_c=p+2\\)-bit two’s-complement words, real before imaginary, most significant bit first, in increasing coordinate order.</p><p class=\"li\">An approximation to \\(L\\) has error \\(E\\) when \\(2^p\\|\\widetilde Lu-Lu\\|_\\infty&lt;E\\) on all permitted grid inputs.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  (P_su)_j=u_{tj},\\qquad(P_tv)_k=v_{-sk}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Citations to Harvey–van der Hoeven’s Gaussian-resampling results refer to the 45-page author-hosted manuscript specified in the paper’s reference [15].</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are linear contractions \\(A:\\mathbb C^s\\to\\mathbb C^t\\) and \\(B_0:\\mathbb C^t\\to\\mathbb C^s\\) such that\n\\[\nP_sF_s=2^{2\\alpha^2}B_0P_tF_tA.\n\\]\nFixed multitape algorithms\n\\[\n\\widetilde A:\\mathbb D_p^s\\to\\mathbb D_p^t,\\qquad\n\\widetilde B_0:\\mathbb D_p^t\\to\\mathbb D_p^s\n\\]\neach have error less than \\(p^2\\) and time\n\\[\nO(tp^{3/2+\\delta}\\alpha),\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(p,s,t,\\alpha\\), without executing the two coordinate permutations.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the Gaussian maps\n\\[\n(Su)_k=\\frac1\\alpha\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}\ne^{-\\pi\\alpha^{-2}(j-sk/t)^2}u_j,\n\\]\n\\[\n(Tu)_k=\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}\ne^{-\\pi\\alpha^2(tj/s-k)^2}u_j.\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nq_j=\\left\\lfloor tj/s+\\tfrac12\\right\\rfloor,\\qquad\n\\beta_j=tj/s-q_j,\n\\]\n\\[\n(Cv)_j=v_{q_j},\\qquad\n(Du)_j=e^{\\pi\\alpha^2\\beta_j^2}u_j,\\qquad N=CTD.\n\\]\nThe diagonal term of \\(N\\) is the identity. The cited Theorem 4.2 and Lemma 4.6 give\n\\[\nTP_sF_s=P_tF_tS,\\qquad\n\\|N-I\\|&lt;2.01e^{-\\pi\\alpha^2\\theta/2}&lt;2^{-\\alpha^2\\theta}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\alpha^2\\theta&gt;p/\\alpha^2&gt;1\\), \\(N\\) is invertible. Set\n\\[\nS'=S/2,\\qquad J'=N^{-1}/2,\\qquad\nD'=2^{-(2\\alpha^2-2)}D.\n\\]\nThe cited Proposition 4.7(i) gives\n\\[\n\\|S'\\|&lt;3/4,\\qquad\\|J'\\|&lt;7/8,\\qquad\\|D'\\|&lt;1.\n\\]\nTake \\(A=S'\\), \\(B_0=D'J'C\\). Since \\(CT=ND^{-1}\\),\n\\[\n(DN^{-1}C)T=I.\n\\]\nApply this left inverse to the Gaussian identity. The normalization product is \\(2^{-2\\alpha^2}\\), giving the stated formula.</p><p>The cited Lemmas 4.8, 4.9, and 4.12 provide ordered disk-grid algorithms:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{map}&amp;\\text{error}&amp;\\text{time}\\\\ \\hline\n\\widetilde S'&amp;16p&amp;O(tp^{3/2+\\delta}\\alpha)\\\\\n\\widetilde J'&amp;3p^2/4&amp;O(tp^{3/2+\\delta}\\alpha)\\\\\n\\widetilde D'&amp;4&amp;O(tp^{1+\\delta}).\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe selection \\(C\\) is an ordered subsequence because \\(q_0=0\\), \\(q_{s-1}&lt;t\\), and \\(q_{j+1}&gt;q_j\\).</p><p>Generate selected indices using \\(t=hs+\\rho\\) and \\(tj=sf_j+e_j\\), \\(0\\le e_j&lt;s\\):\n\\[\nq_j=f_j+[2e_j\\ge s],\n\\]\n\\[\nc_j=[e_j+\\rho\\ge s],\\quad\ne_{j+1}=e_j+\\rho-c_js,\\quad\nf_{j+1}=f_j+h+c_j.\n\\]\nThis costs \\(O(p)\\) per index and \\(O(tp)\\) in total. Thus\n\\[\n\\widetilde A=\\widetilde S',\\qquad\n\\widetilde B_0=\\widetilde D'\\widetilde J'C.\n\\]\nContractions add errors:\n\\[\n16p&lt;p^2,\\qquad4+3p^2/4&lt;p^2.\n\\]</p><p>For representation, implement cited scalar boundaries by sign, ordinary binary magnitude, and separators, with real before imaginary and coordinate order unchanged. Each numerator has at most \\(p+1\\) magnitude bits. Convert to fixed-width two’s complement by padding, complementing and incrementing when negative; invert by recovering magnitude and removing leading zeros. A reusable unary ruler of \\(p+2\\) cells is prepared in \\(O(p)\\) time by successive doubling. Each adapter costs \\(O(p)\\) per scalar and changes no represented integer or numerical error.</p><p>The \\(S'\\) routine visits a periodic window of radius \\(\\lceil\\sqrt p\\rceil\\alpha\\) per output, with tape movement \\(O(tp\\sqrt p\\,\\alpha)\\), including cyclic boundary jumps.</p><p>For \\(E=N-I\\), put \\(k=\\lceil p/(\\alpha^2\\theta)\\rceil\\). The cited Lemma 4.11 computes disk-grid iterates with error less than \\(p/3\\), and its proof gives\n\\[\n\\|\\widetilde Ez\\|&lt;0.42\\|z\\|+(p/3)2^{-p}&lt;1.\n\\]\nThe Neumann procedure begins with the rounded \\(u/2\\) and applies \\(\\widetilde E\\) repeatedly. Since \\(k\\le\\alpha^2+1&lt;p+1\\), its exact signed partial sums require only\n\\[\np+\\lceil\\log_2(k+1)\\rceil+O(1)=O(p)\n\\]\nbits. The row accumulators likewise use \\(O(p)\\) bits.</p><p>One \\(\\widetilde E\\) call has window radius \\(m=\\lceil\\sqrt p/(2\\alpha)\\rceil\\) and movement\n\\[\nO(tp(1+\\sqrt p/\\alpha)).\n\\]\nOver \\(k-1\\) calls this is \\(O(tp\\sqrt p\\,\\alpha)\\). Outer accumulation \\(O(ksp)\\) fits the same bound. Windows exceeding one period are handled by \\(O(1+w/s)\\) period traversals, costing \\(O(wp)\\) per output. A fixed number of tapes holds the current iterate, next iterate, and running sum.</p><p>The original resampling implementation sorts to execute \\(P_t\\) and \\(P_s^{-1}\\). Here those permutations remain in the identity, and the ordered numerical maps retain their original truncations.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma 20, “Tensor interface.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The exact identity is \\( RF_{\\boldsymbol s}=2^\\gamma B_0QF_{\\boldsymbol t}A. \\) Both \\(A,B_0\\) are contractions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Every pair \\((s_i,t_i)\\), \\(1\\le i\\le d\\), satisfies Proposition 9 with the same \\(p,\\alpha,\\delta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  T=\\prod_it_i,\\qquad\\gamma=2d\\alpha^2,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  R=\\bigotimes_iP_{s_i},\\quad Q=\\bigotimes_iP_{t_i},\\quad\n  A=\\bigotimes_iA_i,\\quad B_0=\\bigotimes_iB_{0,i},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  F_{\\boldsymbol q}=\\bigotimes_iF_{q_i},\\qquad\n  F_{\\boldsymbol q}^+=\\bigotimes_iF_{q_i}^+.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The exact identity is\n\\[\nRF_{\\boldsymbol s}=2^\\gamma B_0QF_{\\boldsymbol t}A.\n\\]\nBoth \\(A,B_0\\) are contractions. Their linewise implementations each have error less than \\(dp^2\\), and both maps’ one-dimensional algorithm costs total\n\\[\nO(dTp^{3/2+\\delta}\\alpha).\n\\]</p><p>If \\(t_i\\in\\{r/2,r\\}\\), \\(r=2^\\ell\\), the full padded box is retained, and entries use \\(O(p)\\) bits, exposing and restoring all coordinate lines costs\n\\[\nO(Tp\\,d^2(1+\\ell^\\tau)).\n\\]\nAny fixed polynomial in \\(p\\) of setup per line is negligible relative to \\(Tp\\) when \\(r\\) exceeds every fixed polynomial in \\(p\\) and \\(d\\) is polynomially bounded in \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Tensor Proposition 9’s exact identity.</p><p>For \\(A\\), embed the input into the full \\(t_i\\)-box by zero padding. Expand axes one at a time. After expanding a set \\(I\\), valid coordinates have ranges \\(t_i\\) for \\(i\\in I\\), \\(s_i\\) otherwise. Expose the next axis as innermost; on each line with other coordinates valid, apply \\(\\widetilde A_i\\) to its first \\(s_i\\) entries and write \\(t_i\\) outputs. Write zero lines otherwise.</p><p>For \\(B_0\\), begin with the full box valid. Compress one axis at a time. On each line whose other coordinates remain valid, apply \\(\\widetilde B_{0,i}\\), write its \\(s_i\\) outputs, then \\(t_i-s_i\\) zeros. Invalid lines are consumed and rewritten as zero. The box size remains fixed.</p><p>Each exact axis map is a contraction on its valid lines and zero elsewhere. Taking the maximum line norm and error gives error less than \\(p^2\\) per axis and less than \\(dp^2\\) after composition. At most \\(T/t_i\\) lines invoke the algorithm, so\n\\[\n\\sum_i(T/t_i)O(t_ip^{3/2+\\delta}\\alpha)\n=O(dTp^{3/2+\\delta}\\alpha).\n\\]\nPadding and zero scans fit this bound.</p><p>The descriptor records logical axis names under physical moves. Adjacent axes have widths \\(\\ell\\) or \\(\\ell-1\\); Proposition 3 exchanges equal widths, and a single-bit move handles the excess leading bit of unequal widths. Each exchange costs \\(O(Tp(1+\\ell^\\tau))\\). Exposing and restoring each axis uses \\(O(d)\\) exchanges, hence \\(O(d^2)\\) altogether.</p><p>Since \\(t_i\\ge r/2\\), at most \\(2dT/r\\) lines are processed per tensor map. Setup \\(O(p^C)\\) per line totals\n\\[\nO(dTp^C/r)=o(Tp).\n\\]\nGrowing descriptors and loop indices reside on tapes, not additional heads.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Lemma 21, “Chirp and permutation cancellation.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The chirp is periodic in coordinate \\(i\\) with period \\(t_i\\), and \\( QF_{\\boldsymbol t}u =\\overline a\\cdot \\frac{a*(\\overline a\\cdot u)}T. \\) Also …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 10’s \\(R,Q,s_i,t_i\\), with every \\(t_i\\) even.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(S=\\prod_is_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(j\\in\\mathbb Z^d\\), define\n  \\[\n  a_j=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\pi i\\sum_i\\frac{s_ij_i^2}{t_i}\\right).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(*\\) be cyclic convolution on the product coordinate groups, and let \\(\\cdot\\) mean coordinatewise multiplication.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The chirp is periodic in coordinate \\(i\\) with period \\(t_i\\), and\n\\[\nQF_{\\boldsymbol t}u\n=\\overline a\\cdot\n\\frac{a*(\\overline a\\cdot u)}T.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\n(RF_{\\boldsymbol s}^+)\n\\bigl[(RF_{\\boldsymbol s}u)\\cdot(RF_{\\boldsymbol s}v)\\bigr]\n=\\frac{u*v}{S^2}.\n\\]\nConjugating the input and output of an algorithm for \\(RF_{\\boldsymbol s}\\) gives one for \\(RF_{\\boldsymbol s}^+\\), with the same error bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>Replacing \\(j_i\\) by \\(j_i+t_i\\) changes the chirp exponent by\n\\[\n-\\pi is_i(2j_i+t_i),\n\\]\nan integral multiple of \\(2\\pi i\\).</p><p>The coefficient of \\(u_k\\) in the chirp formula is\n\\[\n\\frac1T\\overline a_j a_{j-k}\\overline a_k\n=\\frac1T\\exp\\!\\left(2\\pi i\\sum_i\\frac{s_ij_ik_i}{t_i}\\right).\n\\]\nThis is the coefficient of \\(QF_{\\boldsymbol t}\\), since \\(Q\\) reads frequency \\((-s_ij_i)_i\\) from the negative-sign transform.</p><p>For a unit \\(c\\bmod q\\), put \\((R_cz)_j=z_{cj}\\). Substituting \\(h=ck\\) gives\n\\[\nF_q^+R_c=R_c^{-1}F_q^+.\n\\]\nTensoring yields \\(F_{\\boldsymbol s}^+R=R^{-1}F_{\\boldsymbol s}^+\\), hence\n\\[\nRF_{\\boldsymbol s}^+R=F_{\\boldsymbol s}^+.\n\\]\nThe common permutation commutes with pointwise multiplication. Therefore the left side of the second identity is\n\\[\nF_{\\boldsymbol s}^+\n(F_{\\boldsymbol s}u\\cdot F_{\\boldsymbol s}v).\n\\]\nUsing\n\\[\nF_{\\boldsymbol s}(u*v)\n=S(F_{\\boldsymbol s}u)\\cdot(F_{\\boldsymbol s}v),\n\\qquad\nF_{\\boldsymbol s}^+F_{\\boldsymbol s}=I/S\n\\]\ngives \\((u*v)/S^2\\).</p><p>Conjugation changes the Fourier sign, commutes with the real permutation \\(R\\), and preserves grid and norm.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Sections 9.1–9.3, including equations (38)–(42)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "These parameters satisfy all layer, transform, and resampling conditions beyond one fixed cutoff.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\) be the supplied operand bit length.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix\n  \\[\n  \\tau=\\sigma=1-2^{-50},\\quad\\beta=\\tfrac12,\\quad\\delta=\\tfrac1{16},\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  \\lambda=1-2^{-52},\\quad c=2^{-56},\\quad\n  \\lambda'=1-2^{-54},\\quad\n  C_1=20,\\quad\\epsilon=2^{-75},\\quad\\kappa=2^{-182}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For sufficiently large \\(n\\), put\n  \\[\n  b=\\lceil\\log_2n\\rceil,\\quad p=6b,\\quad\n  d=\\lfloor b^\\epsilon\\rfloor,\\quad K=\\lfloor d^c\\rfloor.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Take the power of two \\(T\\in[4n/b,8n/b)\\), and put\n  \\[\n  r=2^{\\lceil(\\log_2T)/d\\rceil},\\qquad\\ell=\\log_2r.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(d\\ell-\\log_2T\\) axes of length \\(r/2\\), the others of length \\(r\\), choosing \\(t_d=r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\alpha=\\lceil(12d^2b)^{1/4}\\rceil,\\qquad\n  \\gamma=2d\\alpha^2,\\qquad\\eta=\\frac1{4d}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These parameters satisfy all layer, transform, and resampling conditions beyond one fixed cutoff. Distinct odd primes \\(s_i\\) can be generated with\n\\[\n(1-2\\eta)t_i&lt;s_i\\le(1-\\eta)t_i,\\qquad\nT/2&lt;S:=\\prod_is_i&lt;T.\n\\]\nPrime search costs \\(n^{o(1)}=o(n)\\); the remaining scalar setup is polynomial in \\(p\\).</p><p>For\n\\[\nP_i=\\prod_{j&lt;i}s_j,\\qquad\\mu_i=P_i^{-1}\\pmod{s_i},\n\\]\nthe algebra isomorphism\n\\[\n\\Phi:\\mathbb C[x]/(x^S-1)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_d]/(x_1^{s_1}-1,\\ldots,x_d^{s_d}-1),\n\\qquad\nx\\longmapsto\\prod_ix_i^{\\mu_i}\n\\]\nand its inverse have explicit padded-box implementations of cost\n\\[\nO(Tp\\,d^2(1+\\ell^\\tau))\n\\]\nfor \\(O(p)\\)-bit coefficient records. They preserve cyclic convolution.</p><p>Writing each operand in radix \\(2^b\\), with \\(q=\\lceil n/b\\rceil\\) digits, gives a product polynomial of degree less than \\(S\\), with coefficients\n\\[\n0\\le c_j\\le q(2^b-1)^2&lt;2^{3b}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 12.1–12.3 give the parameter conditions, generated prime lengths, and explicit algebra-preserving layout.</p><p>All remaining scalar setup—fixed rational powers, products, inverses, comparisons, and descriptors—is polynomial in \\(p\\). For example, \\(d=\\lfloor b^{2^{-75}}\\rfloor\\) is found by binary search using exact comparisons \\(d^{2^{75}}\\le b\\); the exponent is fixed.</p><p>The radix product degree satisfies\n\\[\n2q-2&lt;2n/b\\le T/2&lt;S,\n\\]\nso cyclic convolution modulo \\(x^S-1\\) has no nonzero wraparound. Since \\(q\\le n\\le2^b\\),\n\\[\n0\\le c_j\\le q(2^b-1)^2&lt;2^{3b}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Sections 9.1 and 9.2.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The constants satisfy Proposition 5’s inequalities, and \\( Tp=\\Theta(n),\\quad d=\\Theta(p^\\epsilon),\\quad K=\\Theta(p^{\\epsilon c}),\\quad \\ell=\\Theta(p^{1-\\epsilon}),\\quad r=2^{\\Theta(p^{1-\\epsilon})}. \\) Uniform …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 12’s fixed constants and size parameters.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The constants satisfy Proposition 5’s inequalities, and\n\\[\nTp=\\Theta(n),\\quad d=\\Theta(p^\\epsilon),\\quad\nK=\\Theta(p^{\\epsilon c}),\\quad\n\\ell=\\Theta(p^{1-\\epsilon}),\\quad\nr=2^{\\Theta(p^{1-\\epsilon})}.\n\\]\nUniform comparison constants may be fixed as\n\\[\na_d=a_r=1/12,\\quad b_d=1,\\quad b_r=1/3,\\quad C_\\ell=1.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n2\\le\\alpha&lt;\\sqrt p,\\qquad\n\\gamma&lt;28d^2\\sqrt b\\le28b^{2/3}=o(b)\n\\]\neventually; for example \\(b\\ge2^{24}\\) implies \\(\\gamma\\le b/4\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The layer inequalities follow from\n\\[\n\\tau(1+c/\\beta)&lt;1-31\\cdot2^{-55}&lt;\\lambda,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sigma+\\beta(1-\\sigma)=1-2^{-51}&lt;\\lambda&lt;\\lambda'&lt;1.\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\n\\epsilon C_1&lt;1,\\quad\n\\epsilon(2-\\tau)&lt;1-\\tau,\\quad\n\\tfrac34+\\delta+\\tfrac32\\epsilon&lt;1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\epsilon(1+c)&lt;1,\\qquad\\epsilon+\\delta&lt;1.\n\\]</p><p>The integer \\(d\\ell-\\log_2T\\) belongs to \\([0,d)\\), so at least one axis has length \\(r\\), and \\(\\prod_it_i=T\\).</p><p>For sufficiently large \\(b\\),\n\\[\nb/2\\le\\log_2T\\le b.\n\\]\nTogether with \\(p=6b\\), \\(d=\\lfloor b^\\epsilon\\rfloor\\), this gives\n\\[\np^\\epsilon/12\\le d\\le p^\\epsilon,\\qquad\np/(12d)\\le\\ell\\le p/(3d),\n\\]\nand the displayed asymptotics. In particular, \\(r\\) exceeds every fixed polynomial in \\(p\\), \\(K/\\log p\\to\\infty\\), and \\(\\ell/K\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>Using \\(\\lceil A\\rceil\\le2A\\) for \\(A\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n\\alpha\\le2(12d^2b)^{1/4},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\gamma=2d\\alpha^2&lt;28d^2\\sqrt b\n\\le28b^{1/2+2\\epsilon}\\le28b^{2/3}.\n\\]\nThe remaining claims follow.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "Section 9.2.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Distinct odd primes \\(s_i\\) satisfying Proposition 12’s intervals exist and can be generated deterministically in \\(o(n)\\) time.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use Proposition 12’s \\(p,d,r,t_i,\\alpha,\\eta\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Distinct odd primes \\(s_i\\) satisfying Proposition 12’s intervals exist and can be generated deterministically in \\(o(n)\\) time. Every \\((s_i,t_i)\\) satisfies Proposition 9.</p>", "proof": "<p>The cited prime-interval bound, based on Rosser–Schoenfeld estimates, states that for \\(0&lt;\\eta&lt;1/4\\), \\(x&gt;e^{2/\\eta}\\), the interval\n\\[\n(1-2\\eta)x&lt;q\\le(1-\\eta)x\n\\]\ncontains at least \\(\\eta x/(2\\log x)\\) primes.</p><p>For \\(x=r/2\\) or \\(r\\),\n\\[\n\\log(r/2)/d=\\Theta(p^{1-2\\epsilon})\\to\\infty,\n\\]\nso \\(\\log x&gt;8d=2/\\eta\\). The prime count is \\(x/(8d\\log x)&gt;d\\) eventually. The two intervals are disjoint and lie above two. Choose the required distinct odd primes; each is coprime to its power-of-two \\(t_i\\).</p><p>Strict Bernoulli gives\n\\[\nS/T&gt;(1-1/(2d))^d&gt;1/2,\n\\]\nwhile \\(S&lt;T\\). For \\(\\theta_i=t_i/s_i-1\\),\n\\[\n\\theta_i&gt;\\eta,\\qquad\n\\alpha^4\\theta_i&gt;\n\\frac{12d^2b}{4d}=3db\\ge6b=p.\n\\]\nLemma 12.1 gives \\(2\\le\\alpha&lt;\\sqrt p\\), and \\(t_i\\le r\\le T&lt;2^p\\). Hence Proposition 9 applies.</p><p>Scan the two intervals and trial-divide candidates through their square roots. The conservative cost is\n\\[\nO(dr^{3/2}\\operatorname{poly}(p)).\n\\]\nIts logarithm is \\(O(\\log p+p/d)=o(p)\\), so it is \\(n^{o(1)}=o(n)\\). The primes are generated by the machine, not supplied as advice.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.3", "orig": "Section 9.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The coordinate of \\(\\Phi(x^k)\\) in axis \\(i\\) is \\( b_i=a_i+\\mu_i\\sum_{j<i}a_jP_j\\pmod{s_i}. \\) The map, its inverse, and their padding are implemented in \\( O(Tp\\,d^2(1+\\ell^\\tau)) \\) time, preserving the valid source …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 12’s pairwise coprime \\(s_i\\), padded lengths \\(t_i\\), \\(P_i\\), and \\(\\mu_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Every ordinary index has a unique mixed-radix expression\n  \\[\n  k=\\sum_{j=1}^d a_jP_j,\\qquad0\\le a_j&lt;s_j.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The coordinate of \\(\\Phi(x^k)\\) in axis \\(i\\) is\n\\[\nb_i=a_i+\\mu_i\\sum_{j&lt;i}a_jP_j\\pmod{s_i}.\n\\]\nThe map, its inverse, and their padding are implemented in\n\\[\nO(Tp\\,d^2(1+\\ell^\\tau))\n\\]\ntime, preserving the valid source box and zero padding.</p>", "proof": "<p>The image of \\(x\\) has order \\(S\\), since the \\(\\mu_i\\) are units and the \\(s_i\\) are pairwise coprime. Its powers enumerate all target basis monomials exactly once, so \\(\\Phi\\) is an algebra isomorphism.</p><p>For its coordinate formula, terms with \\(j&gt;i\\) are divisible by \\(s_i\\), and \\(\\mu_iP_i=1\\bmod s_i\\).</p><p>Numerical order of \\(k\\) is lexicographic order of \\((a_d,\\ldots,a_1)\\). Scan the full padded box in that order, copying the next original coefficient at addresses with all \\(a_i&lt;s_i\\), and writing zero elsewhere. The valid addresses occur in the original coefficient order. Nested binary counters and bounds have total width \\(O(p)\\), so insertion and deletion each cost \\(O(Tp)\\).</p><p>Reorder the axes to \\((a_1,\\ldots,a_d)\\). Update targets in order \\(d,d-1,\\ldots,1\\). Lower-index controls then still have their original values. For fixed controls, rotate only \\([0,s_i)\\) by the displayed offset and fix \\([s_i,t_i)\\); use the identity if a lower control is invalid. Every update is a bijection preserving both the valid box and its complement.</p><p>Invert by processing \\(i=1,\\ldots,d\\), subtracting the expression formed from already recovered lower coordinates, then undo the axis order and delete padding.</p><p>Each update splits its valid target interval into two consecutive pieces and merges them in the opposite order, copying the invalid interval unchanged. There are at most \\(T/t_i\\) prefixes. Polynomial offset setup totals\n\\[\nO(dTp^C/r)=o(Tp).\n\\]\nReversing the axes takes \\(O(d^2)\\) adjacent exchanges. Proposition 3, with a single-bit adjustment for widths differing by one, costs \\(O(Tp(1+\\ell^\\tau))\\) per exchange.</p><p>This \\(\\Phi\\) describes payload movement to new addresses; it is distinct from the Fourier convention \\((R_cz)_j=z_{cj}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Sections 9.4–9.5 and 9.7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a fixed-tape computed source transform \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal F}\\), with exact counterpart \\( \\mathcal F=2^\\gamma B_0QF_{\\boldsymbol t}A =RF_{\\boldsymbol s}, \\) defined on source disk-grid tensors of norm at …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the explicitly generated parameters of Proposition 12, beyond one common cutoff.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(L=\\log_2T\\), \\(h_p=2^{-p}\\), \\(w_c=p+2\\), and \\(M=T/r\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Scalar arrays use signed \\(w_c\\)-bit component words.</p><p class=\"li\">Interpret the last scalar coordinate as a degree-less-than-\\(r\\) polynomial by\n  \\[\n  (\\iota f)_j=\\sum_{k&lt;r}f_{j,k}y^k.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define normalized complex cyclic convolution by\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal M_{\\mathbb C}(f,g)=\\frac{f*g}{T}\n  \\]\n  on all \\(d\\) scalar axes.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A,B_0,Q,R\\) be Proposition 10’s maps.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a fixed-tape computed source transform \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal F}\\), with exact counterpart\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=2^\\gamma B_0QF_{\\boldsymbol t}A\n=RF_{\\boldsymbol s},\n\\]\ndefined on source disk-grid tensors of norm at most \\(0.5\\), stored in the full padded box.</p><p>It returns a disk-grid tensor in width \\(w_c\\), zero outside valid source coordinates, with error less than\n\\[\nE_sh_p,\\qquad E_s=9\\,2^\\gamma TL.\n\\]\nIts output norm is less than \\(0.501\\). The conjugate procedure\n\\[\n\\widetilde{\\mathcal F}^{+}(z)\n=\\overline{\\widetilde{\\mathcal F}(\\overline z)}\n\\]\nhas exact counterpart \\(RF_{\\boldsymbol s}^{+}\\) and the same guarantees.</p><p>Its costs are accounted for by:\n\\[\nTp[dK+pK^{\\tau-1}+\\ell d^{\\lambda'}+\\log(rp)],\n\\]\n\\[\nTp\\,d^2(1+\\ell^\\tau),\\qquad\ndTp^{3/2+\\delta}\\alpha,\\qquad\nTdp^{1+\\delta},\n\\]\nup to fixed constant factors and lower-order setup.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply the linewise approximation to \\(A\\), then Lemma 13.2, then the linewise approximation to \\(B_0\\), and finally multiply grid numerators exactly by \\(2^\\gamma\\).</p><p>For input norm at most \\(0.5\\), the expansion output has norm less than\n\\[\n0.5+dp^2h_p&lt;0.51,\n\\]\nso it is a legal chirp input. The chirp output is in the disk, so all compression calls are defined and return disk-grid outputs.</p><p>Insert the exact contractions \\(A,QF_{\\boldsymbol t},B_0\\) at the computed inputs. Before scaling, error is less than\n\\[\n(2dp^2+8TL)h_p.\n\\]\nAfter scaling it is less than\n\\[\n2^\\gamma(2dp^2+8TL)h_p&lt;E_sh_p,\n\\]\nbecause \\(2dp^2&lt;TL\\) eventually.</p><p>The exact source transform is a contraction, so the scaled approximation has norm less than\n\\[\n0.5+E_sh_p&lt;0.501.\n\\]\nThus exact scaling does not require clipping. Append \\(\\gamma\\) zeros to each numerator, obtaining width \\(w_c+\\gamma\\), then remove only leading sign-extension bits to restore \\(w_c\\). This costs \\(O(Tp)\\), changes no value, and preserves padded zeros.</p><p>Conjugation gives the opposite-sign procedure. The costs are those of Propositions 6, 8, and 10, Lemmas 13.1–13.2, and linear scalar-format scans. One fixed cutoff enforces all disk margins, including\n\\[\ndp^2h_p,\\quad8TLh_p,\\quad E_sh_p,\\quad\n28\\,2^\\gamma T^2Lh_p&lt;10^{-3}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Sections 9.4, 9.4.1, and the complex-convolution estimates in Section 9.7.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The exact identity is \\( \\mathcal M_{\\mathbb C}(f,g) =D_-\\iota^{-1}\\mathcal M_r(\\iota D_+f,\\iota D_+g). \\) This is a bilinear contraction.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 13’s parameters.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\zeta=e^{\\pi i/r}\\) and\n  \\[\n  (D_\\pm f)_{j,k}=\\zeta^{\\pm k}f_{j,k}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The ring-convolution map is \\(\\mathcal M_r\\) from Proposition 8.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The exact identity is\n\\[\n\\mathcal M_{\\mathbb C}(f,g)\n=D_-\\iota^{-1}\\mathcal M_r(\\iota D_+f,\\iota D_+g).\n\\]\nThis is a bilinear contraction.</p><p>For disk-grid inputs, one of norm at most \\(0.7\\), its computed implementation returns a disk-grid array of norm less than \\(0.701\\) and error less than \\(6TLh_p\\). Its twist work costs \\(O(Tp^{1+\\delta})\\), in addition to Proposition 8’s cost.</p>", "proof": "<p>The map\n\\[\n\\Theta:\\mathbb C[x_d]/(x_d^r-1)\\to\\mathcal R_r,\\qquad\nx_d\\mapsto\\zeta y\n\\]\nis an algebra isomorphism because \\((\\zeta y)^r=1\\). It is diagonal on coefficient bases and preserves coefficient norm. If \\(k+l=j+hr\\), \\(h\\in\\{0,1\\}\\), then twisting and negacyclic wrapping contribute\n\\[\n\\zeta^{k+l}(-1)^h=\\zeta^j(\\zeta^r)^h(-1)^h=\\zeta^j.\n\\]\nUntwisting recovers cyclic wrap. Hence the identity and contraction follow.</p><p>Choose disk-grid phase approximations \\(\\widetilde\\theta_k^\\pm\\) to \\(\\zeta^{\\pm k}\\) with error less than \\(2h_p\\), using the cited exponential algorithm in \\(O(p^{1+\\delta})\\) time per factor. Form\n\\[\nf'=Q_p(\\widetilde\\theta^+\\cdot f),\\qquad\ng'=Q_p(\\widetilde\\theta^+\\cdot g),\n\\]\n\\[\nh'=\\widetilde{\\mathcal M}_r(\\iota f',\\iota g'),\\qquad\n\\widetilde{\\mathcal M}_{\\mathbb C}(f,g)\n=Q_p(\\widetilde\\theta^-\\cdot\\iota^{-1}h').\n\\]\nEach dyadic phase product is exact before truncation, using \\(O(p)\\)-bit numerator products.</p><p>For an exact unit phase \\(e\\), disk-grid approximation \\(\\widetilde e\\), and disk operand \\(x\\),\n\\[\n\\|Q_p(\\widetilde ex)-ex\\|\n&lt;(2\\|x\\|+\\sqrt2)h_p&lt;4h_p,\n\\qquad\n\\|Q_p(\\widetilde ex)\\|\\le\\|x\\|.\n\\]\nThus twists preserve the smaller operand norm.</p><p>Proposition 8’s error is bounded here by\n\\[\nE_{\\rm ring}=M(3L+2)&lt;5TL.\n\\]\nThe exact normalized ring convolution has norm at most \\(0.7\\), so its computed result has norm less than\n\\[\n0.7+5TLh_p&lt;0.701.\n\\]\nUntwisting and truncation do not increase this norm.</p><p>Insert the exact normalized map at the computed twisted operands. The two input twists contribute less than \\(4+4\\), the ring computation \\(E_{\\rm ring}\\), and the output untwist less than \\(4\\), in units of \\(h_p\\). Thus total error is less than \\(6TLh_p\\).</p><p>The scalar and polynomial coefficient layouts agree at width \\(w_c\\), so \\(\\iota\\) changes only descriptors. Proposition 8 returns width\n\\[\nw_M=w_c+\\log_2M.\n\\]\nThe norm bound implies numerator magnitudes at most \\(2^p\\). Remove only redundant leading sign bits to restore width \\(w_c\\), exactly and in \\(O(Tp)\\) time.</p><p>Generating twist factors anew for every scalar entry still costs \\(O(Tp^{1+\\delta})\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "Section 9.5 and its chirp estimates in Section 9.7.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The procedure \\( x_0=Q_p(\\overline{\\widetilde a}\\cdot x),\\qquad y_0=\\widetilde{\\mathcal M}_{\\mathbb C}(\\widetilde a,x_0), \\) \\( \\widetilde{\\mathcal C}(x) =Q_p(\\overline{\\widetilde a}\\cdot y_0) \\) returns a disk-grid …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 13’s parameters.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a\\) be Proposition 11’s chirp, and construct a disk-grid array \\(\\widetilde a\\) with\n  \\[\n  \\|\\widetilde a-a\\|_\\infty&lt;2h_p.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(x\\) be a disk-grid target tensor with \\(\\|x\\|_\\infty\\le0.6\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The procedure\n\\[\nx_0=Q_p(\\overline{\\widetilde a}\\cdot x),\\qquad\ny_0=\\widetilde{\\mathcal M}_{\\mathbb C}(\\widetilde a,x_0),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\widetilde{\\mathcal C}(x)\n=Q_p(\\overline{\\widetilde a}\\cdot y_0)\n\\]\nreturns a disk-grid array of norm less than \\(0.701\\), approximating \\(QF_{\\boldsymbol t}x\\) with error less than \\(8TLh_p\\).</p><p>Chirp construction costs \\(O(Tdp^{1+\\delta})\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>At address \\(j\\), compute the chirp phase numerator modulo \\(2r\\) as\n\\[\n-\\sum_i s_ij_i^2(r/t_i)\\pmod{2r}.\n\\]\nAll integers have \\(O(p)\\) bits, and \\(r/t_i\\in\\{1,2\\}\\). Scan the axis descriptor and use the established multiplier for the \\(d\\) squares and products. Each term costs \\(O(p\\log p)=O(p^{1+\\delta})\\); exponent evaluation costs the same. This gives \\(O(Tdp^{1+\\delta})\\), including descriptor scans.</p><p>The phase inequality in Lemma 13.1 gives \\(\\|x_0\\|\\le0.6\\). The kernel \\(\\widetilde a\\) is in the disk, so the smaller-operand convolution condition holds. Thus \\(\\|y_0\\|&lt;0.701\\), and the endpoint phase does not increase it.</p><p>Proposition 11 identifies the exact counterpart:\n\\[\n\\overline a\\cdot\\mathcal M_{\\mathbb C}(a,\\overline a\\cdot x)\n=QF_{\\boldsymbol t}x.\n\\]\nIn units of \\(h_p\\), the kernel approximation contributes less than \\(2\\), the initial phase less than \\(4\\), computed convolution less than \\(6TL\\), and final phase less than \\(4\\). The sum is less than \\(8TL\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Sections 9.6 and 9.7.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The following computation recovers every exact ordinary integer product coefficient, and carry propagation then returns the exact \\(2n\\)-bit product:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 12’s parameters and Proposition 13’s source procedures.</p><p class=\"li\">Let the two \\(n\\)-bit inputs have radix-\\(2^b\\) digits \\(a_j,b_j\\), padded to length \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Scale each digit by \\(2^{-b-2}\\), and apply the algebra-preserving address map \\(\\Phi\\), obtaining source arrays \\(u,v\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Thus \\(u,v\\) are exact \\(p\\)-bit grid arrays of norm less than \\(1/4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The following computation recovers every exact ordinary integer product coefficient, and carry propagation then returns the exact \\(2n\\)-bit product:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Compute \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal F}(u)\\), \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal F}(v)\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Form their exact coordinatewise products, then apply \\(Q_p\\), obtaining \\(z\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Compute \\(\\widetilde{\\mathcal F}^+(z)\\), then multiply its numerators exactly by \\(S\\), retaining denominator \\(2^p\\), obtaining \\(\\widetilde w\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">4. Multiply \\(\\widetilde w\\)’s numerators exactly by \\(2^{2b+4}S\\), retaining denominator \\(2^p\\), and round real values to nearest integers.</p><p class=\"li num\">5. At valid addresses write the rounded coefficient in signed width \\(3b+1\\), and at padded addresses write zero. Apply \\(\\Phi^{-1}\\) with this payload width.</p><p class=\"li num\">6. Propagate radix-\\(2^b\\) carries in increasing coefficient order and serialize exactly \\(2n\\) output bits.</p><p>All source calls satisfy their norm restrictions, and the real values before rounding have error strictly less than \\(1/2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Read stored operands backward in \\(b\\)-bit groups to form increasing digit order, reverse each buffered group internally, and pad the final group with leading zeros. The digit \\(a_j\\) is written with exact real grid numerator\n\\[\na_j2^{p-b-2}\n\\]\nand zero imaginary numerator. Appending zero coefficients and applying \\(\\Phi\\) gives the stated \\(u,v\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nU=RF_{\\boldsymbol s}u,\\quad V_1=RF_{\\boldsymbol s}v,\\quad\n\\widetilde U=\\widetilde{\\mathcal F}(u),\\quad\n\\widetilde V_1=\\widetilde{\\mathcal F}(v).\n\\]\nExact norms are less than \\(1/4\\); computed norms are less than \\(0.26\\). Hence\n\\[\nz=Q_p(\\widetilde U\\cdot\\widetilde V_1)\n\\]\nhas norm at most \\(0.26^2&lt;0.5\\). The identity\n\\[\n\\widetilde U\\widetilde V_1-UV_1\n=(\\widetilde U-U)\\widetilde V_1\n+U(\\widetilde V_1-V_1)\n\\]\ngives\n\\[\n\\|z-UV_1\\|&lt;(2E_s+2)h_p.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(G=RF_{\\boldsymbol s}^+\\). Proposition 11 gives\n\\[\nG(UV_1)=(u*v)/S^2.\n\\]\nIts contraction bound and the opposite computation give\n\\[\n\\|\\widetilde{\\mathcal F}^+(z)-G(UV_1)\\|\n&lt;(3E_s+2)h_p.\n\\]\nAfter the first exact scale by \\(S\\),\n\\[\nw=(u*v)/S,\\qquad\n\\|\\widetilde w-w\\|&lt;E_wh_p,\n\\]\n\\[\nE_w=S(3E_s+2)&lt;28\\,2^\\gamma T^2L.\n\\]\nSince \\(w\\) averages \\(S\\) products, \\(\\|w\\|&lt;1/16\\), and\n\\[\n\\|\\widetilde w\\|&lt;1/16+10^{-3}&lt;1.\n\\]</p><p>The unscaled integer coefficient array in tensor order is \\(2^{2b+4}Sw\\). The two factors \\(S\\) undo different Fourier normalizations. Since \\(S&lt;T&lt;2^b\\), \\(L&lt;b\\), and \\(\\gamma\\le b/4\\) eventually, the final absolute error is less than\n\\[\n2^{2b+4}S\\cdot28\\,2^\\gamma T^2L\\cdot2^{-6b}\n&lt;448b\\,2^{-b+\\gamma}\n\\le448b\\,2^{-3b/4}&lt;\\tfrac12.\n\\]\nThe true values are real integers, so nearest-integer rounding uniquely recovers them.</p><p>The first multiplication by \\(S\\) fits width \\(w_c+b\\); the second fits \\(w_c+2b\\). Appending \\(2b+4\\) zero bits gives width \\(w_c+4b+4\\). These are exact signed encodings with \\(O(p)\\)-bit words. Recover magnitude, inspect the low \\(p\\) bits for rounding, and restore the sign. Proposition 12’s coefficient bound places the result in \\([0,2^{3b})\\), fitting signed width \\(3b+1\\). Imaginary components are consumed, padded positions are written zero, and \\(\\Phi^{-1}\\) restores ordinary coefficient order.</p><p>For carry propagation, put \\(Q_0=2^b\\) and\n\\[\nk_0=0,\\qquad\nd_j=(c_j+k_j)\\bmod Q_0,\\qquad\nk_{j+1}=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{c_j+k_j}{Q_0}\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nThe bound\n\\[\n0\\le k_j\\le q(Q_0-1)&lt;Q_0^2\n\\]\nis preserved because\n\\[\nc_j+k_j\\le q(Q_0-1)^2+q(Q_0-1)\n=qQ_0(Q_0-1).\n\\]\nThe exact invariant is\n\\[\n\\sum_{h&lt;j}c_hQ_0^h\n=\\sum_{h&lt;j}d_hQ_0^h+k_jQ_0^j.\n\\]\nThus the scan determines successive low radix digits. Write digit bits low-to-high, drain the final \\(O(b)\\)-bit carry, and reverse the completed stream. Its length is \\(O(Sb)\\). The product is less than \\(2^{2n}\\), so bits above position \\(2n-1\\) are zero. Remove only those padding bits and produce exactly \\(2n\\) bits, retaining required leading zeroes. The same invariant handles zero operands.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Section 9.8, “The complete time bound.”", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The complete computation, including deterministic setup, prime generation, all layouts, numerical procedures, exact scaling and rounding, carries, serialization, recursion scheduling, and cleanup, uses one fixed finite …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 14’s complete large-input multiplication computation and Proposition 12’s fixed parameters.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(V=Tp=\\Theta(n)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All scalar component formats use \\(\\Theta(p)\\) bits, and every polynomial record retains its full length-\\(r\\) suffix.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The complete computation, including deterministic setup, prime generation, all layouts, numerical procedures, exact scaling and rounding, carries, serialization, recursion scheduling, and cleanup, uses one fixed finite family of tapes and has time\n\\[\nO(Tp\\,p^{1-\\kappa})\n=O(n(\\log n)^{1-\\kappa}).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>After division by \\(Tp\\), the charged costs and powers of \\(p\\) are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{l|c|c}\n\\text{operation}&amp;\\text{normalized cost}&amp;\\text{power}\\\\ \\hline\n\\text{prefix moves and individual rounds}\n&amp;dK&amp;\\epsilon(1+c)\\\\\n\\text{transform-layout chunk exchanges}\n&amp;pK^{\\tau-1}&amp;1-\\epsilon c(1-\\tau)\\\\\n\\text{simultaneous rounds}\n&amp;\\ell d^{\\lambda'}&amp;1-\\epsilon(1-\\lambda')\\\\\n\\text{CRT and axis layouts}\n&amp;d^2(1+\\ell^\\tau)&amp;\\tau+\\epsilon(2-\\tau)\\\\\n\\text{Gaussian line maps}\n&amp;dp^{1/2+\\delta}\\alpha&amp;3/4+\\delta+3\\epsilon/2\\\\\n\\text{chirps, twists, scalar products}\n&amp;dp^\\delta&amp;\\epsilon+\\delta\\\\\n\\text{packed polynomial products}\n&amp;\\log(rp)&amp;1-\\epsilon\\\\\n\\text{final scaling and rounding}\n&amp;p^\\delta&amp;\\text{absorbed above}\\\\\n\\text{input, padding, carries, output}\n&amp;1&amp;\\text{absorbed above}.\n\\end{array}\n\\]</p><p>The seven dominant margins below one are\n\\[\ng_1=1-\\epsilon(1+c),\\quad\ng_2=\\epsilon c(1-\\tau),\\quad\ng_3=\\epsilon(1-\\lambda'),\n\\]\n\\[\ng_4=1-\\tau-\\epsilon(2-\\tau),\\quad\ng_5=\\tfrac14-\\delta-\\tfrac32\\epsilon,\\quad\ng_6=1-\\delta-\\epsilon,\\quad g_7=\\epsilon.\n\\]\nSubstitution gives\n\\[\ng_1&gt;\\tfrac12,\\quad g_2=2^{-181},\\quad\ng_3=2^{-129},\\quad g_4&gt;2^{-51},\n\\]\n\\[\ng_5&gt;\\tfrac18,\\quad g_6&gt;\\tfrac12,\\quad g_7=2^{-75}.\n\\]\nThus \\(\\min_i g_i=2^{-181}=2\\kappa\\).</p><p>The first three costs are Proposition 6’s terms. Its positional layout uses \\(O(p/K)\\) width-\\(K\\) interchanges, each costing \\(O(VK^\\tau)\\). At most \\(\\ell\\) simultaneous rounds each cost \\(O(Vd^{\\lambda'})\\), including basis changes, repair, row padding, grouping, and cleanup.</p><p>Axis exposure and CRT layouts use \\(O(d^2)\\) exchanges, each \\(O(V(1+\\ell^\\tau))\\). CRT rotations cost \\(O(dV)\\), with polynomial offset preparation \\(O(dTp^C/r)=o(Tp)\\).</p><p>For Gaussian maps,\n\\[\n\\sum_i(T/t_i)O(t_ip^{3/2+\\delta}\\alpha)\n=O(dTp^{3/2+\\delta}\\alpha).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\alpha=\\Theta(p^{1/4+\\epsilon/2})\\), division by \\(Tp\\) gives the displayed exponent.</p><p>Chirp construction scans the axis descriptors per scalar and computes \\(d\\) terms in \\(O(dp^{1+\\delta})\\). Twists and scalar products fit the same row. Exact products by \\(S\\) cost \\(O(p\\log p)\\) per entry, hence normalized \\(O(\\log p)=O(p^\\delta)\\). Exact powers-of-two scaling and sign-extension removal are linear scans.</p><p>There are \\(T/r\\) polynomial products. Four established integer products on \\(O(rp)\\) bits per record cost \\(O(Tp\\log(rp))\\). Packing, extraction, wrapping, and copying are linear. These calls use the established multiplier as an ordinary subroutine, not the improved algorithm.</p><p>For setup, fixed branching and recursion depth \\(O(\\log p)\\), together with polynomially many rounds and groups, give at most \\(p^A\\) layer and address-layout visits for a fixed \\(A\\). Polynomial setup \\(p^C\\) per visit costs \\(p^{A+C}=o(Tp)\\).</p><p>At polynomial-record visits, there are at most \\(O(T/r)\\) records per visit, including bounded row padding. Polynomial address work per record over all visits is\n\\[\nO(Tp^{A+C}/r)=o(Tp).\n\\]\nSetup per Gaussian line similarly costs \\(O(dTp^C/r)=o(Tp)\\).</p><p>The address-interchange procedure also handles one-bit payloads: Lemma 3.3 bounds descriptor work by the current child volume using the retained original chunk ranges. Local counter resets do not rescan growing descriptors per data bit.</p><p>For a packed basis change of volume \\(V_{\\rm call}\\), the bad-address fraction is \\(O(d2^{-K})\\). Each exceptional polynomial record adds an \\(O(p)\\)-bit destination key, so the compact bank has length \\(O(V_{\\rm call}d2^{-K})\\). Its \\(O(p)\\) stable radix passes cost \\(O(V_{\\rm call}dp2^{-K})\\). Summing over polynomially many visits gives\n\\[\nO(Vp^{A'}2^{-K})=o(V),\n\\]\nbecause \\(K=\\Theta(p^{\\epsilon c})\\) with \\(\\epsilon c&gt;0\\). Extraction and reinsertion are already charged full scans.</p><p>Parking stacks and reused work tapes charge only current parent streams; no operation repeatedly scans parked ancestors. Growing axis lists and call states are stored data, not additional tapes or alphabet symbols. Proposition 12 bounds global setup and prime search by \\(o(V)\\).</p><p>Every dominant exponent is at most \\(1-2\\kappa\\). Any remaining fixed power of \\(\\log p\\) is eventually at most \\(p^\\kappa\\). Therefore the total is \\(O(Tp\\,p^{1-\\kappa})\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "the Newton approximation procedures in the proof of Corollary 2, Section 10", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are fixed-tape routines with the following guarantees:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  B(p)=p(\\lg p)^{1-\\kappa}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Exact integer products use the multiplier established by Propositions 14–15.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t\\ge1\\), define\n  \\[\n  R_t(z)=2^{-t}\\lfloor2^tz\\rfloor.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A normalized input is stored most significant bit first with a marked beginning; finite missing fractional digits are zero.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are fixed-tape routines with the following guarantees:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. For \\(1\\le c&lt;2\\), target \\(p\\ge1\\), the reciprocal routine returns a dyadic \\(x\\) with\n   \\[\n   |1-cx|\\le2^{-p-1},\\qquad |x-1/c|\\le2^{-p-1}.\n   \\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For \\(1\\le c&lt;4\\), target \\(p\\ge1\\), the square-root approximation routine returns a dyadic \\(x\\) with\n   \\[\n   |x-\\sqrt c|\\le2^{-p-1}.\n   \\]</p><p>Each complete routine costs \\(O(B(p))\\), including prefix reading, truncations, tape returns, and cleanup.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 16.1–16.2 prove convergence against the true normalized \\(c\\), even as the truncated prefix \\(c_t\\) changes.</p><p>A fixed number of scalar registers suffice. Reciprocal intermediates satisfy \\(x\\le3/2\\), \\(u\\le c_tx\\le3/2\\). Root intermediates satisfy \\(x\\le5/2\\), \\(v&lt;3\\), \\(q_t&lt;12\\). Thus only a fixed number of integer guard bits is required. At working precision \\(t\\), retained significands have \\(t+O(1)\\) bits and exact products \\(2t+O(1)\\) bits.</p><p>Propositions 14–15 give multiplication cost \\(O(B(t))\\); additions, shifts, copies, rounding, and head returns cost \\(O(t)\\).</p><p>Each stage starts at the marked beginning and reads only its required input prefix, returning over that prefix. A root stage forms \\(d\\) once in \\(O(t)\\), and every inner reciprocal stage reads only its shorter prefix. Early stages do not rescan a longer whole input.</p><p>For capped doubling targets \\(m_j\\) ending at \\(p\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_j(m_j+5)=O(p),\n\\]\nand monotonicity of \\(\\lg\\) gives\n\\[\n\\sum_jB(m_j+5)\n\\le(\\lg(p+5))^{1-\\kappa}\\sum_j(m_j+5)\n=O(B(p)).\n\\]\nA reciprocal stage uses two products. A root stage uses one reciprocal at its current precision and one further product; the outer geometric sum is again \\(O(B(p))\\).</p><p>The outer registers and reused reciprocal and multiplier work tapes form a fixed finite family with fixed subroutine depth. Mark visited interval ends on fixed tracks and clear only those intervals. This costs a constant multiple of each call’s running time.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "the reciprocal Newton estimate in Section 10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\(|\\varepsilon|\\le1/2\\), then \\( |1-cx^+|<\\varepsilon^2+6\\delta. \\) The seed \\(x=5/8\\), one warm-up at \\(t=6\\), and capped precision doubling with five guard bits yield Proposition 16’s reciprocal guarantee.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(1\\le c&lt;2\\), \\(c_t=R_t(c)\\), \\(\\delta=2^{-t}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Given a dyadic \\(x\\), compute exact products followed by\n  \\[\n  u=R_t(c_tx),\\qquad x^+=R_t(x(2-u)).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\varepsilon=1-cx\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If \\(|\\varepsilon|\\le1/2\\), then\n\\[\n|1-cx^+|&lt;\\varepsilon^2+6\\delta.\n\\]\nThe seed \\(x=5/8\\), one warm-up at \\(t=6\\), and capped precision doubling with five guard bits yield Proposition 16’s reciprocal guarantee.</p>", "proof": "<p>The hypothesis gives \\(0&lt;x\\le3/2\\). Write\n\\[\n\\eta_1=u-c_tx,\\qquad\n\\eta_2=x^+-x(2-u),\\qquad|\\eta_i|&lt;\\delta.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n1-cx^+\n=\\varepsilon^2-c(c-c_t)x^2+cx\\eta_1-c\\eta_2.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\ncx^2=(1-\\varepsilon)^2/c\\le9/4,\\quad\ncx\\le3/2,\\quad c&lt;2,\n\\]\n\\[\n|1-cx^+|\\le\\varepsilon^2+\\tfrac{23}{4}\\delta\n&lt;\\varepsilon^2+6\\delta.\n\\]</p><p>The seed has residual at most \\(3/8\\); the \\(t=6\\) step has residual less than\n\\[\n9/64+6/64=15/64&lt;1/4.\n\\]\nStart \\(m=1\\). While \\(m&lt;p\\), put\n\\[\nm'=\\min\\{2m,p\\},\\qquad t=m'+5.\n\\]\nIf the residual is at most \\(2^{-m-1}\\), the next residual is at most\n\\[\n2^{-2m-2}+6\\cdot2^{-m'-5}\n\\le\\tfrac7{16}2^{-m'}&lt;2^{-m'-1}.\n\\]\nSet \\(m=m'\\). At completion, divide residual error by \\(c\\ge1\\) for the absolute reciprocal error.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.2", "orig": "the square-root Newton estimate in Section 10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The update satisfies \\( |x^+-\\sqrt c| \\le\\frac{(x-\\sqrt c)^2}{2x}+\\frac92\\delta. \\) The seed \\(x=3/2\\), one warm-up at \\(t=6\\), and the same capped doubling schedule yield Proposition 16’s square-root guarantee.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(1\\le c&lt;4\\), \\(c_t=R_t(c)\\), \\(\\delta=2^{-t}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(1/2\\le x\\le5/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize \\(x=2^jd\\), where \\(1\\le d&lt;2\\), \\(j\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Apply Lemma 16.1 at target \\(t\\) to obtain \\(z\\) with \\(|1-dz|\\le2^{-t-1}\\), and put \\(v=2^{-j}z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Compute\n  \\[\n  q_t=R_t(c_tv),\\qquad\n  x^+=R_t((x+q_t)/2).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The update satisfies\n\\[\n|x^+-\\sqrt c|\n\\le\\frac{(x-\\sqrt c)^2}{2x}+\\frac92\\delta.\n\\]\nThe seed \\(x=3/2\\), one warm-up at \\(t=6\\), and the same capped doubling schedule yield Proposition 16’s square-root guarantee.</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(d\\ge1\\) and \\(2^{-j}\\le2\\),\n\\[\n|v-1/x|\\le\\delta.\n\\]\nBecause \\(c_t&lt;4\\) and \\(1/x\\le2\\),\n\\[\n|q_t-c/x|\n\\le\\delta+c_t|v-1/x|+|c_t-c|/x\n\\le7\\delta.\n\\]\nThe exact Newton identity gives\n\\[\n|x^+-\\sqrt c|\n\\le\\frac{(x-\\sqrt c)^2}{2x}\n+\\delta+\\tfrac12|q_t-c/x|.\n\\]</p><p>At seed \\(3/2\\), initial error is at most \\(1/2\\), so the quadratic term is at most \\(1/12\\). One \\(t=6\\) step has error less than\n\\[\n1/12+5/64&lt;1/4.\n\\]\nThe invariant \\(|x-\\sqrt c|\\le2^{-m-1}\\), \\(m\\ge1\\), keeps\n\\[\n3/4\\le x\\le9/4.\n\\]\nHence \\(1/(2x)\\le1\\), and \\(9/2&lt;6\\). The same capped doubling argument as Lemma 16.1 gives the target error.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [4, 0, "cite"], [4, 10, "cite"], [4, 12, "cite"], [4, 13, "cite"], [4, 14, "cite"], [5, 4, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 4, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [7, 13, "cite"], [7, 16, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [9, 7, "cite"], [10, 3, "cite"], [10, 14, "cite"], [10, 19, "cite"], [10, 25, "cite"], [10, 30, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 10, "cite"], [13, 35, "cite"], [14, 3, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [14, 16, "cite"], [14, 17, "cite"], [15, 19, "cite"], [15, 28, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [18, 15, "cite"], [19, 23, "cite"], [19, 31, "cite"], [19, 35, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 19, "part"], [22, 7, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 31, "cite"], [23, 32, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [24, 29, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [25, 31, "cite"], [26, 33, "cite"], [26, 34, "cite"], [27, 0, "cite"], [27, 31, "cite"], [27, 34, "cite"], [27, 35, "cite"], [28, 27, "cite"], [28, 29, "cite"], [29, 27, "cite"], [30, 27, "cite"], [31, 34, "cite"], [32, 31, "cite"], [32, 33, "cite"], [33, 31, "cite"], [34, 0, "cite"], [34, 35, "cite"], [34, 36, "cite"], [35, 0, "cite"], [35, 36, "cite"], [36, 1, "cite"], [37, 36, "cite"], [37, 38, "cite"], [38, 36, "cite"]]});
